## A tile program as data: `TileProg`, the SSA record a recording handler
## keeps of one run of a kernel body, and `trace_kernel`, that handler. The
## text renderer (render.dawn) and the bytecode writer (knife 3) are its two
## consumers; the golden pins the text, `tileiras` pins the bytes.
#
# Handles are the SSA names of the trace: a counter the handler owns, starting
# at 1 (0 is the entry token). Every operation that produces a value takes the
# next number, so a body that issues the same operations in the same order
# records the same program, which is the assertion this module exists for:
# a kernel traced twice is equal, and its text is the golden's.
#
# Memory operations are ordered by a linear token chain (tile-backend-design
# 5.1): the handler holds the current token in a state cell, every load and
# store consumes it and produces the next, and `MakeToken(0)` at the head of
# `ops` is where the chain starts. Nothing else orders them; Tile IR gives
# program order no meaning between memory operations.
use std/list
use std/map
use std/str
use std/gpu.{Gpu, F64, F32, F16, I32, I64, Dtype, alloc, upload, download, with_gpu_fake, reference_kernels, launch_entry3, dtype_name, EntryArg, Entry1, Entry2, Entry3, Entry4, Entry5,
  entry1, entry2, entry3, entry4, entry5, arg_in, arg_in_whole, arg_out, arg_shared, entry_grid }
use dev.{Dev, Tile, Param, Hints, RedId, IdF, IdI, AssumePred, DivBy, SameElements, Bounded, d_assert, d_print, assume_div_by,
  assume_same_elements, assume_in_range, param, block_id, tile_at, load, store, gather, scatter, atomic_rmw, atomic_add,
  atomic_cas, addf, mul, f_const, i_const, lanes, lt_i, idx_const, idx_add, idx_mul, idx_eq, d_for, d_for2, d_range, d_loop,
  d_reduce, d_if, maxf, gt, d_global, global_ptrs, load_ptrs, store_ptrs, ptr_offset, ptrs, int_to_ptr, ptr_to_int,
  tensor_view, partition_view, load_view, store_view, NoPadding, PadNegInf, extract, insert, powi, d_return_if,
  float_to_float, sub, add_i, d_fork2, select, lt, arange, mmaf, rounding_name, Overflow, Padding, PadZero, padding_name,
  padding_is_special, is_float_dtype, DYN_DIM, idx_of, exp, div, load_cell, load_at, store_cell, store_sub, zeros, fill,
  retile, reduce_sum, reduce_max, reduce_min, scan_sum, broadcast, reshape, tail_mask, for_arch, hint_latency }
use std/narrow.{Rounding}

## One SSA operation of a tile program. Every `dst` / `tok_out` is a handle
## this operation defines; every other handle field refers to an earlier
## definition. `param` is a position in `TileProg.params`; `n` is a tile
## width; `dtype` is a std/gpu dtype name. `IdxConst` / `IdxAdd` / `IdxMul`
## are scalar i32 values: a host constant, a sum, a product.
##
## `For` is a region (tile-backend-design 5.2): a loop over the index handles
## `[lower, upper)` by `step`, with the induction variable `iv` and the
## carried values entering as `inits` (handles defined before the loop),
## named `carried` inside `body` and `results` after it, the three lists
## the same length. `body` is the region's own operations in issue order,
## `Continue(values)` last: the values the next iteration receives, one per
## carried value. The recording handler carries the memory token through
## every loop as the LAST carried value, so `inits`, `carried` and `results`
## each end in a token handle and `Continue` ends in the body's last token.
## `iv`, `carried` and everything `body` defines are visible inside the
## region only; `results` are the loop's values after it.
##
## `Loop` is the other iterating region (knife T5): the same carried
## values as a `For` and no induction variable or bounds, because its trip
## count is not known when it opens. Its body ends in
## `If([], cond, [Break(values)], [Yield([])])` followed by
## `Continue(values)`: `break` hands the loop its final values and
## `continue` hands the next iteration its values, and the `if` between
## them is the only place a `Break` appears. What `Break` carries is what
## ENTERED the iteration (the `carried` handles and the token as it stands
## when the body ends), and what `Continue` carries is what the body
## computed. `Break`'s values, `Continue`'s values, `inits`, `carried` and
## `results` are all the same length, and all five end in a token.
##
## `Return` ends the kernel for the tile block that reaches it, and the one
## place it is recorded is `If([], cond, [Return], [Yield([])])` (knife
## T17): an early exit taken where `cond` holds. It carries nothing, the
## token included, because nothing after it runs. Bytecode 13.4 lets that
## `if` sit in a `Loop`'s body as well as at the top of the entry or inside
## another `if`; never under a `For`, a `Reduce` or a `Scan`.
##
## `Insert` is `Extract`'s other direction (knife T16): `src` is the
## subtile of shape `sub`, `dest` the tile of shape `whole` it goes into,
## and `dst` the tile that answers, `dest` with one subtile replaced.
## `PowI` is the float power with an INTEGER exponent tile (knife T16):
## `base` is a `dtype` tile and `exp` an `exp_dtype` one of the same shape.
##
## `Extract`, `Cat` and `Permute` are knife T2's shape operations, and
## `NumBlocks` the grid's extent (`BlockId`'s companion). `JoinToks` merges
## two or more tokens into one, and it is the one operation here that makes
## the token chain a GRAPH: every other memory operation takes the token
## before it and answers the next, so the record's order is a line.
##
## `MakePtrs`, `PtrAdd`, `PtrInt`, `IntPtr`, `PtrCast`, `LoadAt` and
## `StoreAt` are the pointer surface. A `Load` builds its own address
## ladder in the lowering and never names it; these seven name it:
## `MakePtrs` is the parameter spread over a tile, `PtrAdd` an `offset` by
## an i32 tile the body computed, and the three conversions are the
## dialect's `ptr_to_int`, `int_to_ptr` and `ptr_to_ptr`. `LoadAt` and
## `StoreAt` are `Load` and `Store` through such a tile, and both sit in
## the token chain exactly as `Load` and `Store` do.
##
## `GetGlobal` is the other producer of a pointer tile (knife T7): the
## address of a module-level `Global`, spread over `shape` the way
## `MakePtrs` spreads a parameter's. What it names is a `Global` of the
## same `TileProg` and not an operation, which is why it carries a symbol
## and not a handle.
##
## `Alloca` is the third producer of a pointer tile (knife T10): a fresh
## automatic allocation of `num_elem` elements of `dtype`, aligned to
## `align` bytes and spread over `shape` the same way. Unlike `GetGlobal`
## it names nothing: each occurrence is its own allocation, and the
## dialect limits the lifetime to the block the operation sits in.
## `shared` is the `global` unit attribute.
##
## `Assert`, `Assume` and `Print` are knife T6's three. `Assert` defines
## nothing and is not in the token chain: it is the one operation here that
## can make a launch fail. `Assume` defines a value equal to its operand and
## carries a predicate attribute nothing checks. `Print` IS in the token
## chain (`print_tko`), so it carries `tok_in` and defines `tok_out` the way
## a store does, and its `args` are all of one format and shape, which is
## what the typed surface allows.
##
## `Spread` widens a rank-0 tile to `n` lanes. `Reduce` and `If` are the two
## other regions. A `Scan` is a `Reduce` that keeps every dimension and
## carries one attribute more (`reverse`): its results have the shape of
## its operands. A `Reduce` over `k` operands folds them along `dim` with
## one identity each; `args` are the `2 * k` rank-0 handles the body sees
## (accumulator then element, per operand, in operand order) and `results`
## the `k` rank-0 handles after it; `body` ends in `Yield(values)`, the new
## accumulators. An `If` holds TWO regions, `on_true` and `on_false`, each
## ending in its own `Yield`; `results` are the handles after it. `n` is 0
## for a rank-0 tile everywhere it appears.
##
## `CellViewOf`, `ReshapeOf` and `BroadcastOf` are the batch's PR-1
## (docs/tile-backend-design.md 6.25). `CellViewOf` is the tensor view a
## parameter's CELLS are read and written through: `TensorViewOf` with no
## base (a parameter's cells start at its first element) and one field
## more, `align`. A non-zero `align` is a claim that the parameter's
## address is a multiple of that many bytes, which the lowering issues as
## `assume div_by<align>` on the parameter itself, ahead of the view; the
## recording sets it for every parameter it cuts into cells, In and Out
## (since PR-2; PR-1 set it for an In only). `ReshapeOf`
## regroups a tile's lanes into another shape of as many, and `BroadcastOf`
## widens the dimensions of length 1; `Spread` stays the rank-0 case.
pub type TileOp =
  | MakeToken(dst: Int)
  | BlockId(dst: Int, axis: Int)
  | Load(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, base: Int, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int], mask: Option[Int], pad: Option[Int], tok_in: Int, hints: Hints)
  | Store(tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, base: Int, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int], value: Int, mask: Option[Int], tok_in: Int, hints: Hints)
  | Gather(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, index: Int, shape: List[Int], mask: Option[Int], pad: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | Scatter(tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, index: Int, shape: List[Int], value: Int, mask: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | AtomicRMW(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, mode: String, index: Int, shape: List[Int], value: Int, mask: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | AtomicCAS(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, index: Int, shape: List[Int], cmp: Int, value: Int, mask: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | ConstF(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], value: Float)
  | ConstI(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], value: Int)
  | Iota(dst: Int, n: Int)
  | Lanes(dst: Int, base: Int, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int])
  | UnaryF(dst: Int, op: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | BinaryF(dst: Int, op: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | Fma(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int, acc: Int)
  | MmaF(dst: Int, dtype: String, m: Int, k: Int, n: Int, lhs: Int, rhs: Int, acc: Int)
  | MmaFScaled(dst: Int, dtype: String, scale: String, m: Int, k: Int, n: Int, block: Int,
    lhs: Int, rhs: Int, acc: Int, lhs_scale: Int, rhs_scale: Int)
  | MmaI(dst: Int, m: Int, k: Int, n: Int, lhs: Int, rhs: Int, acc: Int)
  | CmpF(dst: Int, pred: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | CmpI(dst: Int, pred: String, shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | UnaryI(dst: Int, op: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | BinaryI(dst: Int, op: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | Convert(dst: Int, op: String, from: String, to: String, shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | Repack(dst: Int, op: String, from: String, to: String, from_shape: List[Int], to_shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | Select(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], cond: Int, lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | IdxConst(dst: Int, value: Int)
  | IdxAdd(dst: Int, lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | IdxMul(dst: Int, lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | For(iv: Int, lower: Int, upper: Int, step: Int, unsigned: Bool, inits: List[Int], carried: List[Int], results: List[Int], body: List[TileOp])
  | Loop(inits: List[Int], carried: List[Int], results: List[Int], body: List[TileOp])
  | Break(values: List[Int])
  | Return
  | Continue(values: List[Int])
  | Spread(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | Reduce(results: List[Int], dim: Int, shape: List[Int], identities: List[RedId], operands: List[Int], args: List[Int], body: List[TileOp])
  | Scan(results: List[Int], dim: Int, reverse: Bool, shape: List[Int], identities: List[RedId], operands: List[Int], args: List[Int], body: List[TileOp])
  | If(results: List[Int], cond: Int, on_true: List[TileOp], on_false: List[TileOp])
  | Yield(values: List[Int])
  | Extract(dst: Int, dtype: String, from: List[Int], to: List[Int], src: Int, indices: List[Int])
  | Insert(dst: Int, dtype: String, sub: List[Int], whole: List[Int], src: Int, dest: Int, indices: List[Int])
  | PowI(dst: Int, dtype: String, exp_dtype: String, shape: List[Int], base: Int, exp: Int)
  | Cat(dst: Int, dtype: String, dim: Int, lhs_shape: List[Int], rhs_shape: List[Int], lhs: Int, rhs: Int)
  | Permute(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], perm: List[Int], src: Int)
  | NumBlocks(dst: Int, axis: Int)
  | JoinToks(dst: Int, toks: List[Int])
  | MakePtrs(dst: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int])
  | PtrAdd(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], ptrs: Int, offs: Int)
  | PtrInt(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], ptrs: Int)
  | IntPtr(dst: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], ints: Int)
  | PtrCast(dst: Int, from: String, to: String, shape: List[Int], ptrs: Int)
  | LoadAt(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], ptrs: Int, mask: Option[Int], pad: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | Assert(shape: List[Int], cond: Int, message: String)
  | Assume(dst: Int, pred: AssumePred, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], src: Int)
  | Print(tok_out: Int, fmt: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], args: List[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | StoreAt(tok_out: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int], ptrs: Int, value: Int, mask: Option[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | GetGlobal(dst: Int, sym: String, dtype: String, shape: List[Int])
  | Alloca(dst: Int, dtype: String, num_elem: Int, align: Int, shared: Bool, shape: List[Int])
  | TensorViewOf(dst: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, base: Int, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int],
    dyn_shape: List[Int], dyn_strides: List[Int])
  | StridedViewOf(dst: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, tile_shape: List[Int],
    traversal: List[Int], dim_map: List[Int], padding: Padding)
  | GatherViewOf(dst: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, tile_shape: List[Int], sparse_dim: Int,
    padding: Padding)
  | AtomicRedViewOf(tok_out: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, indices: List[Int], mode: String,
    scope: String, value: Int, tok_in: Int)
  | PartitionViewOf(dst: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, tile_shape: List[Int], dim_map: List[Int],
    padding: Padding)
  | LoadView(dst: Int, tok_out: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, indices: List[Int], tok_in: Int)
  | StoreView(tok_out: Int, dtype: String, view: Int, indices: List[Int], value: Int, tok_in: Int)
  | TensorShapeOf(dst: Int, view: Int, dim: Int)
  | IndexSpaceShapeOf(dst: Int, view: Int, dim: Int)
  | CellViewOf(dst: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, align: Int, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int],
    dyn_shape: List[Int], dyn_strides: List[Int])
  | ReshapeOf(dst: Int, dtype: String, from: List[Int], to: List[Int], src: Int)
  | BroadcastOf(dst: Int, dtype: String, from: List[Int], to: List[Int], src: Int)
derive Show

## How deep loops may nest. A kernel body that recurses through `d_for`
## unrolls at recording time, one region per call, and this is where the
## handler stops it (tile-backend-design 5.2).
pub const MAX_LOOP_DEPTH: Int = 16

## How many handles one recording may mint. A helper that recurses without a
## loop unrolls into a flat record that never ends; this is where the handler
## stops that.
pub const MAX_HANDLES: Int = 65536

## One statically allocated global of the module (knife T7): its symbol
## name, the element format, the values it is initialized with (one per
## element, so the tile type is `tile<len(values) x dtype>` and its rank is
## 1, which GlobalOp's verifier requires) and the alignment in bytes, `0`
## for the dialect's default.
##
## A global is NOT part of the entry: it sits beside it in the module, and
## its lifetime is the module's. What the bytecode carries is a record in
## the Global section (id 6) rather than an instruction in the function
## body, which is why `global` 0x31 is `structural` in
## scripts/tileir-features/features.txt the way `entry` and `module` are.
##
## `is_private` and `constant` are the two fields the Global section's record
## grew at bytecode 13.3 (Ops.td versions GlobalOp's `symbol_visibility` and
## `constant` arguments "13.3", and BytecodeWriter.cpp's writeGlobalSection
## writes four varints per global below that version and six at or above it).
## Knife T8 moved the pin to 13.3 and so this record carries them:
## `is_private` becomes the SymbolVisibility enum value the writer holds
## (bytecode.dawn's VIS_PUBLIC and VIS_PRIVATE) and `constant` says the
## global is read-only.
## Neither changes what a kernel computes here, and neither can be checked
## from the host without `cuModuleGetGlobal`, which the `Gpu` effect does not
## have; scripts/tileir-features/attrs.txt records that as the exemption
## `no-module-symbol-ffi`.
pub type Global = {
  sym: String,
  dtype: String,
  values: List[Float],
  align: Int,
  is_private: Bool,
  constant: Bool,
} derive Show

## A kernel as recorded: its entry name, the dtype name of each parameter in
## order (every parameter is a pointer to that format), the globals its
## module declares in declaration order, its operations in issue order
## (`MakeToken(0)` first), and the `optimization_hints` of the entry itself.
##
## `hints` is empty for every kernel but the ones knife T15 records with
## `trace_kernel(.., hints: hs)`. It sits on the program rather than on an
## operation because that is where the dialect puts it: `optimization_hints`
## is an argument of `entry`, written in the function TABLE and not in the
## body (BytecodeWriter.cpp's `writeFunctionTableSection`).
pub type TileProg = {
  name: String,
  params: List[String],
  globals: List[Global],
  ops: List[TileOp],
  hints: Hints,
} derive Show

fn refuse(name: String, what: String) -> Never =
  panic("tileir: kernel `${name}`: ${what}")

# Every dimension of a tile is a power of two from 1 up; Tile IR shapes are.
# The EMPTY shape is the rank-0 tile (`tile<f64>`, not `tile<0xf64>`): what
# a reduction answers and an `if` branches on.
fn is_pow2(n: Int) -> Bool = n >= 1 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0

fn check_shape(name: String, shape: List[Int]) -> Unit =
  match list.find(shape, d => not is_pow2(d)) {
    Some(d) -> refuse(name, "every tile dimension is a power of two, got ${d} in ${show(shape)}")
    None -> ()
  }

# A tile and the layout it is read with have one stride per dimension.
# The nine modes an `atomic_red_view_tko` takes. `xchg` is the tenth and is
# refused by name: a reduction that answers nothing has nothing to exchange
# (AtomicRedViewTkoOp::verify).
fn red_modes() -> List[String] =
  ["and", "or", "xor", "add", "addf", "max", "min", "umax", "umin"]

# The rules `verifyPartitionViewLike` states about a view's own record: a
# tile of at least one dimension, every dimension a power of two, one
# `dim_map` entry a tile dimension, no tensor dimension mapped twice, and a
# special padding value only over a floating point element format. The half
# that needs the TENSOR VIEW's type is in the lowering, which is where a
# handle's type is known.
fn check_view_record(name: String, dtype: String, tile_shape: List[Int], dim_map: List[Int],
  padding: Padding, what: String) -> Unit = {
  if len(tile_shape) == 0 {
    refuse(name, "a ${what}'s tile shape has no dimensions")
  } else {
    ()
  }
  check_shape(name, tile_shape)
  if len(dim_map) != len(tile_shape) {
    refuse(name, "a dim_map maps all ${len(tile_shape)} tile dimension(s), got ${len(dim_map)} mapping(s)")
  } else {
    ()
  }
  let seen_map = dim_map
  match list.find(range(0, len(dim_map)), k =>
      list.any(range(0, k), j => seen_map[j] == seen_map[k])) {
    Some(k) -> refuse(name, "dim_map maps tensor view dimension ${dim_map[k]} at least twice: ${show(dim_map)}")
    None -> ()
  }
  if padding_is_special(padding) && not is_float_dtype(dtype) {
    refuse(name, "padding value `${padding_name(padding)}` is only allowed on a floating point element type, got ${dtype}")
  } else {
    ()
  }
}

fn check_strides(name: String, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int]) -> Unit =
  if len(shape) != len(strides) {
    refuse(name, "a tile of ${len(shape)} dimension(s) is laid out by ${len(strides)} stride(s)")
  } else {
    ()
  }

# The suffixed names knife T4 added are the same operations under another
# ATTRIBUTE value, spelled the way `shru` already was: one dialect opcode,
# two names, and the attribute is the whole difference. Since knife K2 no
# kernel spells them: dev.dawn's functions take the attribute as a
# defaulted parameter and compose the name, so a combination nothing below
# writes (`addf` rounded down AND flushed to zero) arrives here as a name
# that is not on these lists and is refused like a misspelt one.
# bytecode.dawn's `float_op` / `int_op` / `predicate_value` map them back
# to the opcode and `float_rounding` / `float_flags` / `int_attrs` /
# `cmp_ordering` say what the suffix asked for. `cmpi` has no ordering attribute, so
# `check_int_pred` below keeps the six unprefixed ones.
#
# The operation names and predicates the typed surface spells. An effect
# operation cannot be generic (spec 6.5), so `t_binaryf` and its neighbours
# take the name as a string; this is where a name that is not one of them is
# refused, at the point the kernel is recorded, rather than reaching a
# renderer that would print it and a writer that would have no opcode for it.
fn one_of(names: List[String], op: String) -> Bool = list.any(names, n => n == op)

fn check_unary(name: String, op: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["negf", "absf", "exp", "exp2", "log", "log2", "sqrt", "rsqrt", "tanh", "floor", "ceil",
    "sin", "cos", "tan", "sinh", "cosh", "sqrt_approx"], op) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` is not a unary float operation")
  }

fn check_binary(name: String, op: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["addf", "subf", "mulf", "divf", "maxf", "minf", "pow", "atan2", "remf",
    "addf_neg_inf", "addf_pos_inf", "addf_ftz", "mulf_neg_inf", "mulf_pos_inf", "mulf_ftz",
    "divf_neg_inf", "divf_pos_inf", "maxf_nan", "minf_nan"], op) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` is not a binary float operation")
  }

fn check_pred(name: String, pred: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["eq", "ne", "lt", "le", "gt", "ge", "ueq", "une", "ult", "ule", "ugt", "uge"], pred) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${pred}` is not a comparison predicate")
  }

# `cmpi` has no `comparison_ordering`: integers are totally ordered and
# there is no NaN, so the six unordered names have no meaning there and
# are refused rather than quietly taken for the ordered ones (they would
# encode to the same predicate byte, and nothing below layer 2 would say
# the kernel had asked for something the operation cannot do).
fn check_int_pred(name: String, pred: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["eq", "ne", "lt", "le", "gt", "ge"], pred) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${pred}` is not an integer comparison predicate")
  }

# The integer operation names the typed surface spells. `shri` and `shru`
# are one dialect opcode under two signedness attributes and are two names
# here, because the surface that spells them has to say which.
fn check_unary_int(name: String, op: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["negi", "absi"], op) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` is not a unary integer operation")
  }

fn check_binary_int(name: String, op: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["addi", "subi", "muli", "divi", "remi", "maxi", "mini", "mulhii",
    "andi", "ori", "xori", "shli", "shri", "shru", "addi_nsw", "subi_nuw", "muli_nw"], op) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` is not a binary integer operation")
  }

fn check_convert(name: String, op: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["itof", "ftoi", "ftoi_sat", "ftof", "ftof_zero", "ftof_neg_inf", "ftof_pos_inf", "ftof_away",
    "exti", "extis", "trunci", "bitcast"], op) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` is not a conversion")
  }

## The rounding mode an `ftof` named `op` writes into a tile of `to`, in the
## dialect's spelling. The plain `ftof` takes `nearest_even`, except into
## `f8E8M0FNU`, which does not allow it and takes `zero`; the four suffixed
## names (knife T17) take the mode they name. The renderer spells the mode
## from here, and bytecode.dawn's tests hold the writer's
## `ftof_rounding_of` to it, so the bytes and the text cannot disagree.
pub fn ftof_mode(op: String, to: String) -> String = match op {
  "ftof" -> if to == "f8E8M0FNU" { "zero" } else { "nearest_even" }
  "ftof_zero" -> "zero"
  "ftof_neg_inf" -> "negative_inf"
  "ftof_pos_inf" -> "positive_inf"
  "ftof_away" -> "nearest_away"
  _ -> panic("tileir: `${op}` is not a float-to-float conversion")
}

# A float format's precision (significand bits, the hidden one included)
# and its largest and smallest normal exponents: the three numbers LLVM's
# fltSemantics gives `semanticsPrecision`, `semanticsMaxExponent` and
# `semanticsMinExponent`, which is what the dialect's `isStrictWidening`
# compares.
fn float_semantics(dtype: String) -> (Int, Int, Int) = match dtype {
  "f64" -> (53, 1023, -1022)
  "f32" -> (24, 127, -126)
  "tf32" -> (11, 127, -126)
  "bf16" -> (8, 127, -126)
  "f16" -> (11, 15, -14)
  "f8E4M3FN" -> (4, 8, -6)
  "f8E5M2" -> (3, 15, -14)
  "f8E8M0FNU" -> (1, 127, -127)
  "f4E2M1FN" -> (2, 2, 0)
  _ -> panic("tileir: `${dtype}` is not a float format")
}

# Whether every value of `from` is a value of `to`: at least as many
# significand bits and an exponent range that covers the source's. f16 to
# tf32 is one (eleven bits either way, and a wider range); f16 to bf16 is
# not (bf16 has eight bits), nor is f8E8M0FNU to f32 (its smallest
# exponent is -127, one below binary32's smallest normal one).
fn strict_widening(from: String, to: String) -> Bool = {
  let (fp, fmax, fmin) = float_semantics(from)
  let (tp, tmax, tmin) = float_semantics(to)
  tp >= fp && tmax >= fmax && tmin <= fmin
}

## The rounding modes `ftof` allows from `from` to `to`: Ops.td's FToFOp
## table at bytecode 13.4, read top to bottom with the first matching row
## deciding (`FToFOp::verify` enforces it in that order). 13.3 had two rows,
## "`f8E8M0FNU` takes `zero` or `positive_inf`" and "everything else takes
## `nearest_even`"; 13.4 split the second into five.
##
##   to f8E8M0FNU                          zero, positive_inf
##   to any other float under 16 bits      nearest_even
##   an exact widening                     all five IEEE modes
##   f64 to f32                            all but nearest_away
##   f32 to tf32                           nearest_even, zero, nearest_away
##   any other narrowing                   nearest_even, zero
##
## Knife T17 asked `tileiras` 13.4.92 about every pair of the nine float
## formats in all five modes, 360 cells, and its verifier agreed with this
## table on every one (docs/tile-backend-design.md 6.20). Nineteen cells the
## table allows then fail in code generation with an unnamed `failed to
## compile Tile IR program` -- the directed modes into tf32 and bf16, and
## ties-away into bf16, from the narrower formats -- and are NOT refused
## here: that is this assembler's gap, not the dialect's rule.
pub fn ftof_modes(from: String, to: String) -> List[String] =
  if to == "f8E8M0FNU" {
    ["zero", "positive_inf"]
  } else if to == "f8E4M3FN" || to == "f8E5M2" || to == "f4E2M1FN" {
    ["nearest_even"]
  } else if strict_widening(from, to) {
    ["nearest_even", "zero", "negative_inf", "positive_inf", "nearest_away"]
  } else if from == "f64" && to == "f32" {
    ["nearest_even", "zero", "negative_inf", "positive_inf"]
  } else if from == "f32" && to == "tf32" {
    ["nearest_even", "zero", "nearest_away"]
  } else {
    ["nearest_even", "zero"]
  }

# An `ftof` whose mode the pair of formats does not allow is refused here,
# when the kernel is recorded, rather than by the assembler: the writer
# would encode it without complaint (a mode is one byte) and only `tileiras`
# would say no. The same-format `ftof` is still left to `tileiras`, as it
# was before (float_to_float's comment says why).
fn check_ftof(name: String, op: String, from: String, to: String) -> Unit =
  if str.starts_with(op, "ftof") && from != to {
    let mode = ftof_mode(op, to)
    let allowed = ftof_modes(from, to)
    if list.any(allowed, m => m == mode) {
      ()
    } else {
      refuse(name, "ftof from ${from} to ${to} takes ${join(allowed, " or ")}, not ${mode}")
    }
  } else {
    ()
  }

# The BITS one lane of a tile element format occupies. Two of the fifteen
# formats are sub-byte (`i4` and `f4E2M1FN`, both 13.3) and they are the
# reason this is in bits rather than in bytes: `pack` and `unpack` are the
# only operations whose shapes are computed from a width, and they are the
# only ones a half-byte format reaches. `tf32` is 32 bits in a 32-bit slot
# with thirteen of them unused, which is what PackOp's own verifier says
# (`isa<FloatTF32Type>` -> 32) and not a rounding of it.
fn dtype_bits(name: String) -> Int = match name {
  "i1" -> 1
  "i4" -> 4
  "f4E2M1FN" -> 4
  "i8" -> 8
  "f8E4M3FN" -> 8
  "f8E5M2" -> 8
  "f8E8M0FNU" -> 8
  "i16" -> 16
  "f16" -> 16
  "bf16" -> 16
  "i32" -> 32
  "f32" -> 32
  "tf32" -> 32
  "i64" -> 64
  "f64" -> 64
  _ -> panic("tileir: `${name}` has no element width")
}

# The shape `pack` or `unpack` answers, from the shape it is given. Both
# operations reinterpret a rank-1 tile's BYTES as another element format,
# so the lane count scales by the ratio of the two widths and nothing else
# moves. This is the halving a `pack` of `i4` performs and the doubling its
# `unpack` undoes; scripts/tile-golden's pack-result-shape-unhalved is the
# mutant that removes it.
fn repack_shape(name: String, from: String, to: String, shape: List[Int]) -> List[Int] = {
  let bits = lanes_of(shape) * dtype_bits(from)
  if bits % dtype_bits(to) != 0 {
    refuse(name, "a tile of ${lanes_of(shape)} ${from} lane(s) is not a whole number of ${to} lane(s)")
  } else {
    ()
  }
  [bits / dtype_bits(to)]
}

fn lanes_of(shape: List[Int]) -> Int = list.fold(shape, 1, (acc, d) => acc * d)

# `pack` and `unpack` are the two the dialect gives no attribute and a
# SHAPE CHANGE, which is why they are not on `check_convert`'s list: every
# name there answers a tile of the operand's shape. PackOp's and UnpackOp's
# verifiers (one shared `verifyPackUnpackTypes`) say rank 1, different
# element widths, both sides a whole number of bytes and the same number of
# them; the i8 side is fixed by the operation and refused here rather than
# by `tileiras`, because a caller who has the two backwards would otherwise
# read a message about a type it never wrote.
fn check_repack(name: String, op: String, from: String, to: String, shape: List[Int]) -> Unit = {
  if len(shape) != 1 {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` takes a rank-1 tile, got ${len(shape)} dimension(s)")
  } else {
    ()
  }
  let (wide, packed) = if op == "pack" { (from, to) } else { (to, from) }
  if packed != "i8" {
    refuse(name, "`${op}`'s byte side is an `i8` tile, got `${packed}`")
  } else if wide == "i8" {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` needs two different element widths; `bitcast` is the 8-bit one")
  } else if lanes_of(shape) * dtype_bits(from) % 8 != 0 {
    refuse(name, "`${op}` of ${lanes_of(shape)} ${from} lane(s) is not a whole number of bytes")
  } else {
    ()
  }
}

# The read-modify-write modes the dialect defines, spelled as its own
# assembly does. `add` is the integer sum and `addf` the float one: two
# modes rather than one, because AtomicRMWMode is one enum over both and
# the caller has to say which. A mode outside this list is a caller's
# misspelling and is refused when the kernel is recorded, the way an
# operation name is.
# The three names below the ten are one mode under another MEMORY ORDERING
# and MEMORY SCOPE (knife T4): an `atomic_rmw_tko` carries three required
# enum attributes and the writer wrote two of them as constants until then.
# `weak` is the one ordering the two atomics refuse and is not a name here;
# scripts/tile-golden's atomic-rmw-claims-weak-ordering is what says so.
fn check_rmw_mode(name: String, mode: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["and", "or", "xor", "add", "addf", "max", "min", "umax", "umin", "xchg",
    "add_acquire_tl_blk", "add_release_sys", "add_acq_rel_device"], mode) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${mode}` is not an atomic read-modify-write mode")
  }

fn check_int_dtype(name: String, dtype: String) -> Unit =
  if one_of(["i32", "i8", "i1", "i16", "i64"], dtype) {
    ()
  } else {
    refuse(name, "`${dtype}` is not an integer tile format")
  }

# `extract` answers a subtile whose shape DIVIDES the source's in every
# dimension, and its indices are the slice numbers, one per dimension: both
# come from ExtractOp's verifier, and both are cheaper to refuse here than
# to read out of `tileiras`.
fn check_extract(name: String, from: List[Int], to: List[Int], indices: Int) -> Unit =
  if len(from) != len(to) {
    refuse(name, "extract answers a tile of ${len(to)} dimension(s) from one of ${len(from)}")
  } else if indices != len(from) {
    refuse(name, "extract of a ${len(from)}-dimensional tile takes that many slice indices, got ${indices}")
  } else {
    match list.find(range(0, len(from)), k => to[k] < 1 || from[k] % to[k] != 0) {
      Some(k) -> refuse(name, "extract dimension ${k}: ${to[k]} does not divide ${from[k]} evenly")
      None -> ()
    }
  }

# `insert` is held to `extract`'s three rules with the two shapes' roles
# exchanged: the SOURCE divides the destination (InsertOp's verifier says
# "result dimension .. must be evenly divisible by source dimension"), the
# ranks agree and there is one slice index per dimension. The verifier only
# WARNS about an index it can see is out of bounds, and an index here is a
# handle whose value the recording does not know, so the range of an index
# is left where the dialect leaves it: undefined behaviour.
fn check_insert(name: String, sub: List[Int], whole: List[Int], indices: Int) -> Unit =
  if len(sub) != len(whole) {
    refuse(name, "insert puts a tile of ${len(sub)} dimension(s) into one of ${len(whole)}")
  } else if indices != len(whole) {
    refuse(name, "insert into a ${len(whole)}-dimensional tile takes that many slice indices, got ${indices}")
  } else {
    match list.find(range(0, len(whole)), k => sub[k] < 1 || whole[k] % sub[k] != 0) {
      Some(k) -> refuse(name, "insert dimension ${k}: ${sub[k]} does not divide ${whole[k]} evenly")
      None -> ()
    }
  }

# `fpowi`'s operands: a float base and an exponent of one of the four
# integer formats Ops.td's CudaTile_PowIExponentTileType names. i64 is an
# integer tile format here and NOT one of them, which is why this is its
# own check rather than `check_int_dtype`.
fn check_powi(name: String, dtype: String, exp_dtype: String) -> Unit =
  if not one_of(["f64", "f32", "tf32", "f16", "bf16"], dtype) {
    refuse(name, "powi raises a float tile, and `${dtype}` is not one this package computes in")
  } else if not one_of(["i1", "i8", "i16", "i32"], exp_dtype) {
    refuse(name, "powi's exponent is an i1, i8, i16 or i32 tile, got `${exp_dtype}`")
  } else {
    ()
  }

# `cat` joins two tiles of one rank that agree everywhere but at `dim`.
fn check_cat(name: String, dim: Int, lhs: List[Int], rhs: List[Int]) -> Unit =
  if len(lhs) != len(rhs) {
    refuse(name, "cat joins tiles of one rank, got ${len(lhs)} and ${len(rhs)}")
  } else if dim < 0 || dim >= len(lhs) {
    refuse(name, "cat along dimension ${dim} of a ${len(lhs)}-dimensional tile")
  } else {
    match list.find(range(0, len(lhs)), k => k != dim && lhs[k] != rhs[k]) {
      Some(k) -> refuse(name, "cat: the operands differ at dimension ${k}, ${lhs[k]} and ${rhs[k]}")
      None -> ()
    }
  }

# `permute` wants a rank of at least two (PermuteOp's verifier says so) and
# a `perm` that is a permutation of the dimensions.
fn check_perm(name: String, shape: List[Int], perm: List[Int]) -> Unit =
  if len(shape) < 2 {
    refuse(name, "permute wants a tile of rank two or more, got ${len(shape)}")
  } else if len(perm) != len(shape) {
    refuse(name, "a permutation of a ${len(shape)}-dimensional tile has that many entries, got ${len(perm)}")
  } else {
    match list.find(range(0, len(perm)), k => not list.any(range(0, len(perm)), j => perm[j] == k)) {
      Some(k) -> refuse(name, "the permutation ${show(perm)} does not mention dimension ${k}")
      None -> ()
    }
  }

# An `assume` predicate against the tile it is attached to. Every clause
# here is one of AssumeOp's own verifier's (Attributes.cpp), refused a step
# earlier so that the message names the kernel: a divisor that is not a
# positive power of two, an `every` without its `along`, a `same_elements`
# whose length is not the rank, and bounds the wrong way round.
fn check_assume(name: String, pred: AssumePred, shape: List[Int]) -> Unit = match pred {
  DivBy(divisor, every, along) -> {
    if divisor <= 0 || (divisor & (divisor - 1)) != 0 {
      refuse(name, "assume div_by wants a positive power of two, got ${divisor}")
    } else {
      ()
    }
    match (every, along) {
      (None, None) -> ()
      (Some(e), Some(a)) ->
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          refuse(name, "assume div_by: `every`/`along` cannot be used on a rank-0 tile")
        } else if a < 0 || a >= len(shape) {
          refuse(name, "assume div_by along dimension ${a} of a ${len(shape)}-dimensional tile")
        } else if e < 0 || e > shape[a] {
          refuse(name, "assume div_by every ${e} along a dimension of ${shape[a]}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
      _ -> refuse(name, "assume div_by takes `every` and `along` together or neither")
    }
  }
  SameElements(groups) ->
    if len(groups) != len(shape) {
      refuse(name, "assume same_elements carries one value per dimension, got ${len(groups)} for rank ${len(shape)}")
    } else {
      match list.find(range(0, len(groups)), k => groups[k] < 0 || groups[k] > shape[k]) {
        Some(k) -> refuse(name, "assume same_elements value ${k} is ${groups[k]}, outside 0 to ${shape[k]}")
        None -> ()
      }
    }
  Bounded(lb, ub) -> match (lb, ub) {
    (Some(l), Some(u)) ->
      if l > u { refuse(name, "assume bounded wants a lower bound at most the upper one, got ${l} and ${u}") } else { () }
    _ -> ()
  }
}

# How many format expressions `fmt` holds, by PrintTkoOp's own rule
# (CudaTile.cpp's `extractFormatExpression`): a `%` runs to the first
# conversion character, `%%` is an escaped percent and takes no operand, and
# a `%` that reaches the end of the string with no conversion character is
# malformed.
fn format_args(name: String, fmt: String) -> Int = count_formats(name, fmt, str.chars(fmt), 0, 0)

fn count_formats(name: String, fmt: String, cs: List[String], i: Int, seen: Int) -> Int =
  if i >= len(cs) {
    seen
  } else if cs[i] != "%" {
    count_formats(name, fmt, cs, i + 1, seen)
  } else {
    match conversion_end(cs, i + 1) {
      None -> refuse(name, "print format `${fmt}` ends in an unterminated format expression")
      Some(j) ->
        if cs[j] == "%" {
          count_formats(name, fmt, cs, j + 1, seen)
        } else {
          count_formats(name, fmt, cs, j + 1, seen + 1)
        }
    }
  }

fn conversion_end(cs: List[String], i: Int) -> Option[Int] =
  if i >= len(cs) {
    None
  } else if one_of(["d", "i", "u", "o", "x", "X", "e", "E", "f", "F", "g", "G", "a", "A", "c", "s", "S", "p", "n", "%"], cs[i]) {
    Some(i)
  } else {
    conversion_end(cs, i + 1)
  }

# A memory operation names a declared parameter and reads it as the format it
# was declared with. Both are the body's `Param[D]` against the `params` list
# the caller gave `trace_kernel`, and disagreement is a bug in the caller: the
# entry signature would say one format and the load another.
fn check_param(name: String, params: List[String], pos: Int, dtype: String, verb: String) -> Unit =
  if pos < 0 || pos >= len(params) {
    refuse(name, "${verb} names parameter ${pos}, but ${len(params)} parameter(s) are declared")
  } else if params[pos] != dtype {
    refuse(name, "parameter ${pos} is declared ${params[pos]}, but a ${verb} reads it as ${dtype}")
  } else {
    ()
  }

# An open region: the operations of the enclosing region as they stood when
# it opened, and whatever the closing operation needs to build the region
# around them. One variant per kind of region, because an `if` is two of
# them in a row and a reduction carries what a loop does not.
type Frame =
  | LoopFrame(outer: List[TileOp], iv: Int, lower: Int, upper: Int, step: Int, unsigned: Bool, inits: List[Int], carried: List[Int])
  | WhileFrame(outer: List[TileOp], inits: List[Int], carried: List[Int])
  | ReduceFrame(outer: List[TileOp], dim: Int, shape: List[Int], identities: List[RedId], operands: List[Int], args: List[Int])
  | ScanFrame(outer: List[TileOp], dim: Int, reverse: Bool, shape: List[Int], identities: List[RedId], operands: List[Int], args: List[Int])
  | ThenFrame(outer: List[TileOp], cond: Int)
  | ElseFrame(outer: List[TileOp], cond: Int, on_true: List[TileOp])

# A reduction's, a scan's or an `if`'s region is pure: the dialect requires
# it of the first two and none of the three carries the memory ordering
# token, so a load, a store or a loop (which carries the token through
# itself) inside one has no order to be in. This is what says so: every
# frame but the loop's.
fn is_pure_region(f: Frame) -> Bool = match f {
  LoopFrame(_outer, _iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried) -> false
  WhileFrame(_outer, _inits, _carried) -> false
  _ -> true
}

# Whether a `return` may sit inside the region `f` opened: an `if` (either
# branch) or a `loop`, and since bytecode 13.4 only those two
# (ReturnOp::verify, knife T17).
fn return_passes(f: Frame) -> Bool = match f {
  WhileFrame(_outer, _inits, _carried) -> true
  ThenFrame(_outer, _cond) -> true
  ElseFrame(_outer, _cond, _on_true) -> true
  _ -> false
}

# The next free handle after minting `k` from `next`, or a refusal when the
# recording has grown past MAX_HANDLES.
fn mint(name: String, next: Int, k: Int) -> Int =
  if next + k > MAX_HANDLES {
    refuse(name, "the recording mints more than ${MAX_HANDLES} handles; a helper that recurses unrolls forever")
  } else {
    next + k
  }

fn last(xs: List[Int]) -> Int = xs[len(xs) - 1]

fn but_last(xs: List[Int]) -> List[Int] = list.take(xs, len(xs) - 1)

# ---- the handle table and the fork check (knives C1 and D-1) ----
#
# docs/tile-backend-design.md 6.21 is the long form of what follows.

# An index value as a sum of ATOMS: `konst` plus `coeff * atom` for each
# `(atom, coeff)` in `terms`, where an atom is a handle the arithmetic below
# does not see through (a block id, a loop's induction variable, a value
# read from memory, a product of two non-constant handles). Two handles
# whose forms differ by a constant are a constant apart in every block and
# every iteration; that is the whole of what the fork check asks.
type Lin = {
  terms: List[(Int, Int)],
  konst: Int,
}

# What the recording knows about one handle: the element format it carries
# (`ptr<f64>` for a pointer tile) and its shape (`[]` for rank 0). `what`
# says which of three things the row describes, because a view has a row
# too (knife C1') and a view is not a tile:
#
# - `TileRow`: a value, and the row is its type. A tile read through a view
#   (`load_view`, `load_gather`) is one: the view's tile shape in the view's
#   format, which is what the lowering's `view_tile_of` gives it.
# - `TensorViewRow`: a `tensor_view`. `shape` is the TENSOR's extent along
#   each dimension, `DYN_DIM` where an operand carries it.
# - `GridViewRow(what)`: a partition, strided or gather/scatter view (`what`
#   names which). `shape` is the TILE one `load_view` or `store_view` moves,
#   not the tensor and not the grid.
#
# Tokens are the only handles with no row. A view row is never an operand
# of an element-wise operation (`check_operand` refuses one by what it is),
# and `t_shape_of` answers it as written here.
#
# `affine` is RESERVED for knife D-2 and is `None` in every row this knife
# writes. D-2 fills it with the handle's value as a `Lin` whose atoms are the
# block ids (and loop induction variables), so that each parameter's write
# addresses can be summarized as `A * block + F` when the kernel is recorded
# (research-tile-disjoint-writes 3.2). D-1 below computes the same form on
# demand, from the recorded operations, for the few handles a fork's writes
# name; D-2 is the knife that keeps it per handle.
type Held = {
  dtype: String,
  shape: List[Int],
  affine: Option[Lin],
  what: HeldAs,
}

type HeldAs =
  | TileRow
  | TensorViewRow
  | GridViewRow(what: String)

fn hold(held: Map[Int, Held], h: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  map.insert(held, h, Held { dtype: dtype, shape: shape, affine: None, what: TileRow })

fn hold_view(held: Map[Int, Held], h: Int, what: HeldAs, dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  map.insert(held, h, Held { dtype: dtype, shape: shape, affine: None, what: what })

# What a row is, in a refusal's words.
fn held_noun(x: Held) -> String = match x.what {
  TileRow -> tile_text(x.dtype, x.shape)
  TensorViewRow -> "a tensor view"
  GridViewRow(what) -> "a ${what}"
}

fn ptr_of(dtype: String) -> String = "ptr<${dtype}>"

# `to[k]` gets `from[k]`'s row, for each `k` whose source has one: a loop's
# carried values and results are its inits again, and an `if`'s results are
# what its then branch yields.
fn copy_held(held: Map[Int, Held], from: List[Int], to: List[Int]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  list.fold(range(0, len(to)), held, (m, k) => match map.get(m, from[k]) {
    Some(x) -> map.insert(m, to[k], x)
    None -> m
  })

# A reduction's results, its operands without dimension `dim`; its region
# arguments, rank 0 of each operand's format (two per operand).
fn reduce_held(held: Map[Int, Held], operands: List[Int], dim: Int, results: List[Int]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  list.fold(range(0, len(results)), held, (m, k) => match map.get(m, operands[k]) {
    Some(x) -> hold(m, results[k], x.dtype, list.map(list.filter(range(0, len(x.shape)), j => j != dim), j => x.shape[j]))
    None -> m
  })

fn args_held(held: Map[Int, Held], operands: List[Int], args: List[Int]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  list.fold(range(0, len(args)), held, (m, k) => match map.get(m, operands[k / 2]) {
    Some(x) -> hold(m, args[k], x.dtype, [])
    None -> m
  })

# The values an `if`'s then branch yields: its last operation's.
fn then_yields(on_true: List[TileOp]) -> List[Int] = match on_true[len(on_true) - 1] {
  Yield(values) -> values
  _ -> []
}

## How many operations `ops` holds counted the way `TileProg.ops` reads
## depth first: one for each, plus everything inside a region, its closing
## `Continue` / `Yield` included. Of a whole program's `ops`, one more than
## the largest number a `Call` range or a refusal's `op #k` can name.
pub fn op_count(ops: List[TileOp]) -> Int =
  list.fold(ops, 0, (n, op) => n + 1 + match op {
    For(_iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried, _results, body) -> op_count(body)
    Loop(_inits, _carried, _results, body) -> op_count(body)
    Reduce(_results, _dim, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args, body) -> op_count(body)
    Scan(_results, _dim, _reverse, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args, body) -> op_count(body)
    If(_results, _cond, on_true, on_false) -> op_count(on_true) + op_count(on_false)
    _ -> 0
  })

## The number a refusal gives the operation being recorded: its position in
## `TileProg.ops` read depth first (a region, then its body, then what
## follows it), with the entry's `MakeToken(0)` as #0. So `vadd`'s `addf` is
## #6: the block id, the two index operations of `tile_at` and two loads
## come before it. Every region still open counts once for itself, ahead of
## its body, because that is where the finished program will put it.
fn op_number(frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp]) -> Int = {
  let before = list.fold(frames, 0, (n, f) => n + 1 + match f {
    LoopFrame(outer, _iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried) -> op_count(outer)
    WhileFrame(outer, _inits, _carried) -> op_count(outer)
    ReduceFrame(outer, _dim, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> op_count(outer)
    ScanFrame(outer, _dim, _reverse, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> op_count(outer)
    ThenFrame(outer, _cond) -> op_count(outer)
    ElseFrame(outer, _cond, on_true) -> op_count(outer) + op_count(on_true)
  })
  before + op_count(ops) + 1
}

# A refusal that names the operation as well as the kernel (knife C1,
# research-cutile-rs-api 借-11): `op #k` is `op_number`'s and `op` the
# operation's dialect name. The message has no source position because a
# Dawn panic carries none on either backend (measured, 6.21); the number is
# the cheapest way back to the kernel body.
fn refuse_at(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], op: String, what: String) -> Never =
  refuse(name, "op #${op_number(frames, ops)} `${op}`: ${what}")

fn tile_text(dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> String =
  if len(shape) == 0 {
    "tile<${dtype}>"
  } else {
    "tile<${join(list.map(shape, d => "${d}"), "x")}x${dtype}>"
  }

# One operand of an element-wise operation against what the operation
# declares it to be. A view is refused by what it is; a handle with no row
# (a token, or a number no operation answered) is not compared: the check
# refuses only what it can see is wrong, and every such program is one
# `tileiras` refuses too. Since knife C1' a tile read through a view has a
# row, so it is compared like any other.
fn check_operand(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, role: String, h: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> Unit =
  match tile_row(name, frames, ops, held, op, role, h) {
    Some(x) ->
      if x.dtype != dtype || x.shape != shape {
        refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${role} is ${tile_text(x.dtype, x.shape)}, declared ${tile_text(dtype, shape)}")
      } else {
        ()
      }
    None -> ()
  }

# `h`'s row where a tile is expected: `None` for no row, and a refusal for a
# view's.
fn tile_row(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, role: String, h: Int) -> Option[Held] =
  match map.get(held, h) {
    Some(x) -> match x.what {
      TileRow -> Some(x)
      _ -> refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${role} is ${held_noun(x)}, not a tile")
    }
    None -> None
  }

# One matrix operand of an `mma` against the K it declares (knife C1'): the
# left one is `[m, k]` and K is its dimension 1, the right one `[k, n]` and
# K is its dimension 0. Only K is held here, because K is the dimension the
# two operands must AGREE on; a wrong m or n is a wrong result type, which
# the lowering's types and `tileiras` already say. A handle with no row is
# not compared, as in `check_operand`.
fn check_k(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, role: String, h: Int, axis: Int, k: Int) -> Unit =
  match tile_row(name, frames, ops, held, op, role, h) {
    Some(x) ->
      if len(x.shape) != 2 {
        refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${role} is ${tile_text(x.dtype, x.shape)}, which is not a matrix")
      } else if x.shape[axis] != k {
        refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${role} is ${tile_text(x.dtype, x.shape)}, whose K (dimension ${axis}) is ${x.shape[axis]}, declared k = ${k}")
      } else {
        ()
      }
    None -> ()
  }

# ---- D-1: the two chains a fork joins write no element in common

fn lin_const(v: Int) -> Lin = Lin { terms: [], konst: v }

# `ts` with `coeff` more of `atom`.
fn atom_of(t: (Int, Int)) -> Int = {
  let (x, _c) = t
  x
}

fn bump(ts: List[(Int, Int)], atom: Int, coeff: Int) -> List[(Int, Int)] =
  if list.any(ts, t => atom_of(t) == atom) {
    list.map(ts, t => {
      let (x, c) = t
      if x == atom { (x, c + coeff) } else { (x, c) }
    })
  } else {
    ts ++ [(atom, coeff)]
  }

fn lin_add(a: Lin, b: Lin) -> Lin = {
  let terms = list.fold(b.terms, a.terms, (ts, t) => {
    let (atom, coeff) = t
    bump(ts, atom, coeff)
  })
  Lin {
    terms: list.filter(terms, t => {
      let (_x, c) = t
      c != 0
    }),
    konst: a.konst + b.konst,
  }
}

fn lin_scale(a: Lin, k: Int) -> Lin =
  if k == 0 {
    lin_const(0)
  } else {
    Lin {
      terms: list.map(a.terms, t => {
        let (x, c) = t
        (x, c * k)
      }),
      konst: a.konst * k,
    }
  }

# `h` as a sum of atoms, read off the operations that defined it.
fn lin_of(defs: Map[Int, TileOp], h: Int) -> Lin = match map.get(defs, h) {
  Some(IdxConst(_d, v)) -> lin_const(v)
  Some(IdxAdd(_d, a, b)) -> lin_add(lin_of(defs, a), lin_of(defs, b))
  Some(IdxMul(_d, a, b)) -> {
    let la = lin_of(defs, a)
    let lb = lin_of(defs, b)
    if len(la.terms) == 0 {
      lin_scale(lb, la.konst)
    } else if len(lb.terms) == 0 {
      lin_scale(la, lb.konst)
    } else {
      Lin { terms: [(h, 1)], konst: 0 }
    }
  }
  _ -> Lin { terms: [(h, 1)], konst: 0 }
}

# What one write may touch, as simply as the check needs it: `Span` is the
# elements `base + lo` to `base + hi` of a parameter (the hull of a strided
# tile's lanes, which is exact for a contiguous one); `AnyOf` is somewhere in
# a parameter, at addresses the recording does not follow (a scatter, an
# atomic, a view); `Unnamed` is a write whose target is not a parameter the
# recording can name (an alloca, a global, a pointer made from an integer).
type Write =
  | Span(param: Int, base: Lin, lo: Int, hi: Int)
  | AnyOf(param: Int)
  | Unnamed

# The parameter a pointer tile points into, when it was spread from one and
# only offset or recast since.
fn ptr_param(defs: Map[Int, TileOp], h: Int) -> Option[Int] = match map.get(defs, h) {
  Some(MakePtrs(_d, param, _dtype, _shape)) -> Some(param)
  Some(PtrAdd(_d, _dtype, _shape, ptrs, _offs)) -> ptr_param(defs, ptrs)
  Some(PtrCast(_d, _from, _to, _shape, ptrs)) -> ptr_param(defs, ptrs)
  _ -> None
}

fn view_param(defs: Map[Int, TileOp], h: Int) -> Option[Int] = match map.get(defs, h) {
  Some(TensorViewOf(_d, param, _dtype, _base, _shape, _strides, _ds, _dst)) -> Some(param)
  Some(CellViewOf(_d, param, _dtype, _align, _shape, _strides, _ds, _dst)) -> Some(param)
  Some(PartitionViewOf(_d, _dtype, view, _ts, _dm, _p)) -> view_param(defs, view)
  Some(StridedViewOf(_d, _dtype, view, _ts, _tr, _dm, _p)) -> view_param(defs, view)
  Some(GatherViewOf(_d, _dtype, view, _ts, _sd, _p)) -> view_param(defs, view)
  _ -> None
}

fn named(p: Option[Int]) -> Write = match p {
  Some(param) -> AnyOf(param)
  None -> Unnamed
}

# The lowest and the highest element offset a tile of `shape` laid out by
# `strides` reaches from its base.
fn span_of(param: Int, base: Lin, shape: List[Int], strides: List[Int]) -> Write = {
  let reach = list.map(range(0, len(shape)), k => (shape[k] - 1) * strides[k])
  Span(param, base, list.fold(reach, 0, (n, r) => if r < 0 { n + r } else { n }),
    list.fold(reach, 0, (n, r) => if r > 0 { n + r } else { n }))
}

# The writes one operation performs, at any depth (a loop's body writes
# what it writes).
fn writes_of(defs: Map[Int, TileOp], op: TileOp) -> List[Write] = match op {
  Store(_t, param, _dtype, base, shape, strides, _v, _m, _ti, _h) -> [span_of(param, lin_of(defs, base), shape, strides)]
  StoreAt(_t, _dtype, shape, ptrs, _v, _m, _ti) -> match map.get(defs, ptrs) {
    # `ptr_offset(ptrs(p, ..), lanes(base, shape, strides))` is a store at
    # `base` written the long way, and is read as one
    Some(PtrAdd(_d, _dt, _s, root, offs)) -> match (map.get(defs, root), map.get(defs, offs)) {
      (Some(MakePtrs(_d2, param, _dt2, _s2)), Some(Lanes(_d3, base, lshape, lstrides))) ->
        if lshape == shape { [span_of(param, lin_of(defs, base), lshape, lstrides)] } else { [AnyOf(param)] }
      _ -> [named(ptr_param(defs, ptrs))]
    }
    _ -> [named(ptr_param(defs, ptrs))]
  }
  Scatter(_t, param, _dtype, _i, _s, _v, _m, _ti) -> [AnyOf(param)]
  AtomicRMW(_d, _t, param, _dtype, _mode, _i, _s, _v, _m, _ti) -> [AnyOf(param)]
  AtomicCAS(_d, _t, param, _dtype, _i, _s, _c, _v, _m, _ti) -> [AnyOf(param)]
  StoreView(_t, _dtype, view, _ix, _v, _ti) -> [named(view_param(defs, view))]
  AtomicRedViewOf(_t, _dtype, view, _ix, _mode, _scope, _v, _ti) -> [named(view_param(defs, view))]
  For(_iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried, _results, body) -> writes_in(defs, body)
  Loop(_inits, _carried, _results, body) -> writes_in(defs, body)
  _ -> []
}

fn writes_in(defs: Map[Int, TileOp], body: List[TileOp]) -> List[Write] = {
  let none: List[Write] = []
  list.fold(body, none, (ws, o) => ws ++ writes_of(defs, o))
}

# Every operation of `ops` at any depth, regions before their bodies.
fn flat(ops: List[TileOp]) -> List[TileOp] = {
  let none: List[TileOp] = []
  list.fold(ops, none, (acc, op) => acc ++ [op] ++ match op {
    For(_iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried, _results, body) -> flat(body)
    Loop(_inits, _carried, _results, body) -> flat(body)
    Reduce(_results, _dim, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args, body) -> flat(body)
    Scan(_results, _dim, _reverse, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args, body) -> flat(body)
    If(_results, _cond, on_true, on_false) -> flat(on_true) ++ flat(on_false)
    _ -> none
  })
}

# Everything recorded so far, at any depth: the regions still open
# contribute what they hold.
fn recorded(frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp]) -> List[TileOp] = {
  let none: List[TileOp] = []
  list.fold(frames, none, (acc, f) => acc ++ match f {
    LoopFrame(outer, _iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried) -> flat(outer)
    WhileFrame(outer, _inits, _carried) -> flat(outer)
    ReduceFrame(outer, _dim, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> flat(outer)
    ScanFrame(outer, _dim, _reverse, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> flat(outer)
    ThenFrame(outer, _cond) -> flat(outer)
    ElseFrame(outer, _cond, on_true) -> flat(outer) ++ flat(on_true)
  }) ++ flat(ops)
}

# The token an operation of the chain answers, and the ones it was ordered
# after. A loop answers its last result and was ordered after its last
# init: the token rides through every loop as the last carried value.
fn tok_step(op: TileOp) -> Option[(Int, List[Int])] = match op {
  Load(_d, t, _p, _dt, _b, _s, _st, _m, _pd, ti, _h) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  Store(t, _p, _dt, _b, _s, _st, _v, _m, ti, _h) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  Gather(_d, t, _p, _dt, _i, _s, _m, _pd, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  Scatter(t, _p, _dt, _i, _s, _v, _m, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  AtomicRMW(_d, t, _p, _dt, _mode, _i, _s, _v, _m, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  AtomicCAS(_d, t, _p, _dt, _i, _s, _c, _v, _m, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  LoadAt(_d, t, _dt, _s, _ps, _m, _pd, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  StoreAt(t, _dt, _s, _ps, _v, _m, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  Print(t, _f, _dt, _s, _a, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  AtomicRedViewOf(t, _dt, _v, _ix, _mode, _scope, _val, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  LoadView(_d, t, _dt, _v, _ix, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  StoreView(t, _dt, _v, _ix, _val, ti) -> Some((t, [ti]))
  JoinToks(t, toks) -> Some((t, toks))
  For(_iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, inits, _carried, results, _body) -> Some((last(results), [last(inits)]))
  Loop(inits, _carried, results, _body) -> Some((last(results), [last(inits)]))
  _ -> None
}

# The tokens `tok` is ordered after, itself included, walking the chain
# back until it leaves what was recorded (the entry token, or a token a
# region open around the fork carries in).
fn tok_ancestry(chain: Map[Int, (TileOp, List[Int])], tok: Int, seen: List[Int]) -> List[Int] =
  if list.contains(seen, tok) {
    seen
  } else {
    match map.get(chain, tok) {
      Some((_op, before)) -> list.fold(before, seen ++ [tok], (s, t) => tok_ancestry(chain, t, s))
      None -> seen ++ [tok]
    }
  }

# Why two writes might touch one element, or `None` when they cannot.
fn overlap(a: Write, b: Write) -> Option[String] = match (a, b) {
  (Unnamed, _) -> Some("a write whose target is not a parameter (an alloca, a global, or a pointer made from an integer) cannot be told apart from the other chain's")
  (_, Unnamed) -> Some("a write whose target is not a parameter (an alloca, a global, or a pointer made from an integer) cannot be told apart from the other chain's")
  (AnyOf(p), AnyOf(q)) -> if p != q { None } else { Some("both write parameter ${p} at addresses the recording does not follow") }
  (AnyOf(p), Span(q, _b, _lo, _hi)) -> if p != q { None } else { Some("both write parameter ${p}, one at addresses the recording does not follow") }
  (Span(p, _b, _lo, _hi), AnyOf(q)) -> if p != q { None } else { Some("both write parameter ${p}, one at addresses the recording does not follow") }
  (Span(p, ba, alo, ahi), Span(q, bb, blo, bhi)) ->
    if p != q {
      None
    } else {
      let d = lin_add(ba, lin_scale(bb, 0 - 1))
      if len(d.terms) != 0 {
        Some("both write parameter ${p}, from bases that are not a constant distance apart")
      } else if d.konst + ahi < blo || bhi < d.konst + alo {
        None
      } else {
        Some("both write parameter ${p}: counted from the second chain's base, the first writes elements ${d.konst + alo} to ${d.konst + ahi} and the second ${blo} to ${bhi}")
      }
    }
}

# D-1: the chains `toks` end are unordered against each other wherever
# they do not share history, so a write one of them issued after the fork
# and a write another issued after it must not reach one element. Within
# one block the block id is the same atom on both sides and cancels, which
# is why this is decidable at recording time and needs no grid.
fn check_fork(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], toks: List[Int]) -> Unit = {
  let all = recorded(frames, ops)
  let no_defs: Map[Int, TileOp] = map.empty()
  let defs = list.fold(all, no_defs, (m, op) => list.fold(defined(op), m, (m2, h) => map.insert(m2, h, op)))
  let no_chain: Map[Int, (TileOp, List[Int])] = map.empty()
  let chain = list.fold(all, no_chain, (m, op) => match tok_step(op) {
    Some((t, before)) -> map.insert(m, t, (op, before))
    None -> m
  })
  let none: List[Int] = []
  let ancestry = list.map(toks, t => tok_ancestry(chain, t, none))
  let no_pairs: List[(Int, Int)] = []
  let pairs = list.fold(range(0, len(toks)), no_pairs, (ps, i) => ps ++ list.map(range(i + 1, len(toks)), j => (i, j)))
  let nothing: Option[String] = None
  let why = list.fold(pairs, nothing, (found, pr) => match found {
    Some(_w) -> found
    None -> {
      let (i, j) = pr
      first_overlap(writes_after(chain, defs, ancestry[i], ancestry[j]),
        writes_after(chain, defs, ancestry[j], ancestry[i]))
    }
  })
  match why {
    Some(w) -> refuse_at(name, frames, ops, "join_tokens", "the chains it joins are unordered and may write the same element: ${w}")
    None -> ()
  }
}

# The writes of the chain whose tokens are `mine` that the chain whose
# tokens are `theirs` is not ordered after.
fn writes_after(chain: Map[Int, (TileOp, List[Int])], defs: Map[Int, TileOp], mine: List[Int], theirs: List[Int]) -> List[Write] = {
  let none: List[Write] = []
  list.fold(list.filter(mine, t => not list.contains(theirs, t)), none, (ws, t) => match map.get(chain, t) {
    Some((op, _before)) -> ws ++ writes_of(defs, op)
    None -> ws
  })
}

# The first reason a write of `wi` and one of `wj` might meet.
fn first_overlap(wi: List[Write], wj: List[Write]) -> Option[String] = {
  let nothing: Option[String] = None
  list.fold(wi, nothing, (found, a) => match found {
    Some(_w) -> found
    None -> list.fold(wj, nothing, (f, b) => match f {
      Some(_w) -> f
      None -> overlap(a, b)
    })
  })
}

## Run `body` once under a handler that records what it issues, and answer
## the record. `params` is the dtype name of each entry parameter in order;
## the body's `param(d, pos)` values must agree with it (position in range,
## same format) or the run panics. Two runs of the same body answer equal
## programs.
##
## Regions: `t_loop_begin` pushes the operations recorded so far and opens an
## empty list for the loop body; `t_loop_end` pops, wraps the body in a `For`
## and appends it to what was pushed. The token cell is carried through the
## loop as the last loop-carried value: the body starts from the loop's
## carried token and the code after the loop continues from the loop's token
## result, so a store after a loop is ordered after every store inside it.
## A `t_loop_begin` deeper than MAX_LOOP_DEPTH, a `t_loop_end` with no open
## loop or with the wrong number of values, or a body that ends with a loop
## still open, is refused.
##
## `hints` is the entry's `optimization_hints` (knife T15), empty for every
## kernel but knife T15's. It is the last parameter, after `body`, so that
## the hundreds of `trace_kernel(name, params, () => ..)` calls written
## before it had a default still read the same (knife K2 folded
## `trace_kernel_hinted` into it; docs/std-defaults-design.md 3 says why the
## defaults go after the body).
pub fn trace_kernel(name: String, params: List[String], body: fn() -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints = []) -> TileProg = {
  let (prog, _calls) = trace_calls(name, params, body, hints: hints)
  prog
}

# ---- knife C2: parameter markers with roles and cells, and typed entries
#
# `trace_kernel` takes the parameters' formats as a list of strings because
# Dawn has no variadic generics: the list is the only way to say "this many
# parameters, of these formats" in one signature, and it repeats what the
# body's own `Param[F64]` already says (191 of 191 kernels in
# scripts/tile-golden). `trace1` to `trace5` take one MARKER per parameter
# instead, the way `d_for2` to `d_for4` take one carried value each, and
# compute the list: `In(F64, cells(..))` says the format once, and says the
# two things a string could not (docs/tile-backend-design.md 6.24):
#
# - the ROLE. An In parameter is never written: the recording refuses a
#   store, a scatter, an atomic or a view store into it, and a write
#   through pointers it cannot follow back to a parameter while any
#   parameter is an In. An Out is written one cell per tile block (D-4 will
#   hold the body to that; this knife does not), and a Shared one is the
#   escape hatch, today's `Param`.
# - the CELLS. How the tensor behind the parameter is cut, which for the Out
#   parameters IS the launch grid. Bodies do not read the cells yet (D-4's
#   `load_cell` / `store_cell` will); what this knife does with them is hold
#   them to each other and hand them to std/gpu's entry, so a launch can be
#   held to them.
#
# The markers issue no operation: a kernel recorded through `trace3` is the
# program `trace_kernel` records for the same body, byte for byte.

## The axis entry of a cut dimension that follows no grid axis: the kernel
## picks that cell some other way (the `k` of a matrix product's loop). The
## same number as std/gpu's.
pub const FREE_AXIS: Int = 0 - 1

## How a kernel parameter's tensor is cut. `Whole` is not cut at all.
## `Grid` cuts a row-major tensor of `extent` into cells of `tile`:
##
## - an extent of `DYN_DIM` is the launch's to decide, and is the grid's
##   blocks along the axis that dimension follows times the tile, so the
##   cells then cover the tensor with no tail;
## - `pad` is what a read past the extent answers (the partition view's
##   `padding_value`, a float format's only for the four special ones);
## - `along[j]` is the grid axis dimension `j` follows, or `FREE_AXIS`. A
##   dimension that follows axis `k` has as many cells as the grid has
##   blocks along `k`.
##
## [`cells`] builds one with the defaults; `Whole` and `Grid` are its
## values, for a match.
pub type Cells =
  | Whole
  | Grid(extent: List[Int], tile: List[Int], pad: Padding, along: List[Int])
derive Show

## `extent` cut into cells of `tile`, a read past the extent answering
## `pad`, dimension `k` following grid axis `k` (the first three; a fourth
## and later follow none). Pass `along` for a cut whose dimensions follow
## other axes, or none: the left operand of a matrix product follows axis 0
## along its rows and `FREE_AXIS` along K. An `Out`'s dimensions each follow
## an axis, no two the same one: a batched product's output cut
## `along: [2, 0, 1]` keeps its batch on axis 2.
pub fn cells(extent: List[Int], tile: List[Int], pad: Padding = PadZero,
  along: List[Int] = list.map(range(0, len(tile)), k => if k < 3 { k } else { FREE_AXIS })) -> Cells =
  Grid(extent, tile, pad, along)

## One parameter of a kernel, for `trace1` to `trace5`: its format marker
## (std/gpu's `F64` and so on), what the kernel does to it, and for `In` and
## `Out` how its tensor is cut.
##
## - `In` is read and never written: the recording refuses any write into
##   it. `In(F64, Whole)` is an input read anywhere (a gather's table).
## - `Out` is written, one cell per tile block, and its cells are the
##   launch grid; it may be read (an in-place kernel reads its cell first).
## - `Shared` is read and written in ways no cell describes: atomics,
##   scatters, a stencil's neighbours.
pub type Arg[D] =
  | In(d: D, g: Cells)
  | Out(d: D, g: Cells)
  | Shared(d: D)

# What the recording holds a parameter to: its role without its format.
type Access =
  | Reading
  | Owning
  | Sharing

# The aim of a pointer tile or view that addresses no parameter: a global's
# or an alloca's memory.
const NO_PARAM: Int = 0 - 1

# One parameter's marker with its format as a name, so that five markers of
# five formats fit in one list.
type Sig = {
  dtype: String,
  access: Access,
  g: Cells,
}

fn marker[D](a: Arg[D]) -> D = match a {
  In(d, _g) -> d
  Out(d, _g) -> d
  Shared(d) -> d
}

## A parameter's marker with its format erased, so markers of several
## formats fit in one list: what [`trace_calls`] takes.
pub opaque type Marker = Sig

## `a` as a [`Marker`].
pub fn erase[D: Dtype](a: Arg[D]) -> Marker = {
  let m: Marker = sig_of(a)
  m
}

fn sig_of[D: Dtype](a: Arg[D]) -> Sig = match a {
  In(d, g) -> Sig { dtype: dtype_name(d), access: Reading, g: g }
  Out(d, g) -> Sig { dtype: dtype_name(d), access: Owning, g: g }
  Shared(d) -> Sig { dtype: dtype_name(d), access: Sharing, g: Whole }
}

fn access_word(a: Access) -> String = match a {
  Reading -> "In"
  Owning -> "Out"
  Sharing -> "Shared"
}

# The checks only Tile IR makes of one parameter's cells: each tile
# dimension is a power of two (every tile is), a special padding value is a
# float format's, and an Out's dimensions follow grid axes one to one. The
# rest is arithmetic std/gpu does for the launch too (`entry_grid`).
#
# An Out's `along` is any injection into the grid's three axes (0.8.1):
# dimension j follows axis `along[j]`, and an axis no dimension follows has
# one block, because the grid is the Out's cells (`out_axes` gives std/gpu
# the cut axis by axis, a `1` where nothing follows). So block `b` writes
# cell `(b[along[0]], ..)`, and two blocks that write one cell agree on
# every followed axis and, having one block there, on every other: the
# disjointness the identity gave, kept by an injection. `FREE_AXIS` is not
# one: a dimension no axis decides is not the grid's.
fn check_cells(name: String, k: Int, s: Sig) -> Unit = match s.g {
  Whole -> ()
  Grid(_extent, tile, pad, along) -> {
    let who = "argument ${k} (${access_word(s.access)})"
    match list.find(tile, t => not is_pow2(t)) {
      Some(_t) -> refuse(name, "${who} has a tile of ${show(tile)}; every tile dimension is a power of two")
      None -> ()
    }
    if padding_is_special(pad) && not is_float_dtype(s.dtype) {
      refuse(name, "${who} is ${s.dtype} and pads with ${padding_name(pad)}, which only a float format can hold")
    } else {
      ()
    }
    # a rank past three is std/gpu's to refuse, in the launch's words
    if s.access == Owning && len(along) <= 3 {
      if list.any(along, a => a == FREE_AXIS) {
        refuse(name, "${who} follows grid axes ${show(along)}; every dimension of an Out follows a grid axis, because its cells are the grid")
      } else if list.any(along, a => a < 0 || a > 2) {
        refuse(name, "${who} follows grid axes ${show(along)}; a grid has axes 0, 1 and 2")
      } else if list.any(range(0, len(along)), j => list.any(range(0, j), i => along[i] == along[j])) {
        refuse(name, "${who} follows grid axes ${show(along)}; two of its dimensions follow one axis, so two blocks would write one cell")
      } else {
        ()
      }
    } else {
      ()
    }
  }
}

fn entry_arg(s: Sig) -> EntryArg = match (s.access, s.g) {
  (Reading, Whole) -> arg_in_whole()
  (Reading, Grid(extent, tile, _pad, along)) -> arg_in(extent, tile, along)
  (Owning, Whole) -> arg_out([], [])
  (Owning, Grid(extent, tile, _pad, along)) -> {
    let (e, t) = out_axes(extent, tile, along)
    arg_out(e, t)
  }
  (Sharing, _g) -> arg_shared()
}

# An Out's cut as std/gpu's `arg_out` takes it, whose dimension k is grid
# axis k: axis by axis, the extent and tile of the dimension that follows
# it, or 1 and 1 where none does, the trailing ones dropped. The grid it
# derives is the Out's, and the elements it reaches are as many. A cut of
# rank past three passes as it is, for std/gpu to refuse.
fn out_axes(extent: List[Int], tile: List[Int], along: List[Int]) -> (List[Int], List[Int]) =
  if len(along) > 3 || along == range(0, len(along)) {
    (extent, tile)
  } else {
    let top = list.fold(along, 0, (m, a) => if a + 1 > m { a + 1 } else { m })
    let at = (k: Int) => list.find(range(0, len(along)), j => along[j] == k)
    let e = list.map(range(0, top), k => match at(k) {
      Some(j) -> extent[j]
      None -> 1
    })
    let t = list.map(range(0, top), k => match at(k) {
      Some(j) -> tile[j]
      None -> 1
    })
    (e, t)
  }

# The entry's arguments, every marker checked first: alone (`check_cells`),
# and against one another and the grid (`entry_grid`). Refused before the
# body runs, so a kernel with bad markers records nothing.
fn entry_args(name: String, sigs: List[Sig]) -> List[EntryArg] = {
  for k in range(0, len(sigs)) {
    check_cells(name, k, sigs[k])
  }
  let args = list.map(sigs, entry_arg)
  match entry_grid(args) {
    Err(p) -> refuse(name, p)
    Ok(_grid) -> args
  }
}

fn record_sigs(name: String, sigs: List[Sig], body: fn() -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints) -> TileProg = {
  let (prog, _calls) = record_calls(name, list.map(sigs, s => s.dtype), list.map(sigs, s => s.access),
    list.map(sigs, s => s.g), body, hints)
  prog
}

## `trace_kernel` for a kernel of one parameter, described by its marker
## rather than by a format name; beside the program, the entry std/gpu's
## `launch_entry1` runs. The body receives the `Param` the marker makes, so
## its format is the marker's. A marker whose cells are malformed, or that
## disagree with another's or with the grid, is refused before the body
## runs; a write into an `In` parameter is refused while it records. The
## program is the one `trace_kernel` records for the same body.
pub fn trace1[A: Dtype](name: String, a: Arg[A], body: fn(Param[A]) -> Unit !Dev,
  hints: Hints = []) -> (TileProg, Entry1[A]) = {
  let args = entry_args(name, [sig_of(a)])
  let prog = record_sigs(name, [sig_of(a)], () => body(param(marker(a), 0)), hints)
  (prog, entry1(name, args[0]))
}

## [`trace1`] for two parameters.
pub fn trace2[A: Dtype, B: Dtype](name: String, a: Arg[A], b: Arg[B],
  body: fn(Param[A], Param[B]) -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints = []) -> (TileProg, Entry2[A, B]) = {
  let sigs = [sig_of(a), sig_of(b)]
  let args = entry_args(name, sigs)
  let prog = record_sigs(name, sigs, () => body(param(marker(a), 0), param(marker(b), 1)), hints)
  (prog, entry2(name, args[0], args[1]))
}

## [`trace1`] for three parameters.
pub fn trace3[A: Dtype, B: Dtype, C: Dtype](name: String, a: Arg[A], b: Arg[B], c: Arg[C],
  body: fn(Param[A], Param[B], Param[C]) -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints = []) -> (TileProg, Entry3[A, B, C]) = {
  let sigs = [sig_of(a), sig_of(b), sig_of(c)]
  let args = entry_args(name, sigs)
  let prog = record_sigs(name, sigs,
    () => body(param(marker(a), 0), param(marker(b), 1), param(marker(c), 2)), hints)
  (prog, entry3(name, args[0], args[1], args[2]))
}

## [`trace1`] for four parameters.
pub fn trace4[A: Dtype, B: Dtype, C: Dtype, E: Dtype](name: String, a: Arg[A], b: Arg[B], c: Arg[C],
  d: Arg[E], body: fn(Param[A], Param[B], Param[C], Param[E]) -> Unit !Dev,
  hints: Hints = []) -> (TileProg, Entry4[A, B, C, E]) = {
  let sigs = [sig_of(a), sig_of(b), sig_of(c), sig_of(d)]
  let args = entry_args(name, sigs)
  let prog = record_sigs(name, sigs,
    () => body(param(marker(a), 0), param(marker(b), 1), param(marker(c), 2), param(marker(d), 3)), hints)
  (prog, entry4(name, args[0], args[1], args[2], args[3]))
}

## [`trace1`] for five parameters.
pub fn trace5[A: Dtype, B: Dtype, C: Dtype, E: Dtype, F: Dtype](name: String, a: Arg[A], b: Arg[B],
  c: Arg[C], d: Arg[E], e: Arg[F],
  body: fn(Param[A], Param[B], Param[C], Param[E], Param[F]) -> Unit !Dev,
  hints: Hints = []) -> (TileProg, Entry5[A, B, C, E, F]) = {
  let sigs = [sig_of(a), sig_of(b), sig_of(c), sig_of(d), sig_of(e)]
  let args = entry_args(name, sigs)
  let prog = record_sigs(name, sigs,
    () => body(param(marker(a), 0), param(marker(b), 1), param(marker(c), 2), param(marker(d), 3),
      param(marker(e), 4)), hints)
  (prog, entry5(name, args[0], args[1], args[2], args[3], args[4]))
}

# `aims` after handle `to` was made from `from` (an offset, a recast, a view
# of a view): it aims where `from` does, or nowhere the recording followed.
fn follow(aims: Map[Int, Int], from: Int, to: Int) -> Map[Int, Int] = match map.get(aims, from) {
  Some(p) -> map.insert(aims, to, p)
  None -> aims
}

# The name a write is refused under: the call the kernel made that issued
# it (`store_masked`, `store_view`), or the operation's own name when the
# body called the effect directly.
fn writer(marks: List[Mark], calls: List[Call], op: String) -> String = {
  let row = open_row(marks)
  if row >= 0 { calls[row].name } else { op }
}

# A write into the parameter `aim` names: `Some(p)` for parameter `p`,
# `Some(NO_PARAM)` for memory no parameter addresses, `None` when the
# pointers or the view came from nothing the recording followed (an
# `int_to_ptr`, a value carried through a region). Refused into an In, and,
# when the target is unknown, while any parameter is an In: the recording
# cannot tell that write from one into it.
#
# The batch's D-7: an Out is written through its cells and nothing else.
# `cell` says this write is one (`store_cell` or `store_sub`, through a view
# the recording itself made of the parameter's cells); any other write into
# an Out is refused, and so is a write the recording cannot follow while any
# parameter is an Out. So "each block writes its own cell" is the only thing
# a kernel CAN say of an Out, which is what lets std/gpu's `launch_entryN`
# run the blocks of one launch with no order between them; the escape hatch
# is `Shared`, which takes every write and claims nothing.
fn check_write(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], roles: List[Access],
  aim: Option[Int], op: String, cell: Bool) -> Unit =
  if len(roles) == 0 {
    ()
  } else {
    match aim {
      Some(p) ->
        if p >= 0 && p < len(roles) && roles[p] == Reading {
          refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "parameter ${p} is an In, and nothing writes an In")
        } else if p >= 0 && p < len(roles) && roles[p] == Owning && not cell {
          refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "parameter ${p} is an Out, which is written through its cells alone (store_cell, store_sub); a parameter written any other way is a Shared")
        } else {
          ()
        }
      None -> match list.find(range(0, len(roles)), k => roles[k] == Reading || roles[k] == Owning) {
        Some(p) -> refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "it writes through pointers the recording cannot follow to a parameter (an int_to_ptr, or a value carried through a region), and parameter ${p} is an ${access_word(roles[p])}")
        None -> ()
      }
    }
  }

# ---- the batch's PR-1: the rank-0 rule, the rank-0 checks and cell views
#
# docs/tile-backend-design.md 6.25 is the long form of what follows.

# The bytes a parameter's address is claimed to be a multiple of, on the
# view its cells are read or written through. std/gpu's buffers come from
# `cuMemAlloc`, which aligns to 256, a Tensor has no sub-views, and a
# parameter's cells start at its first element; 16 is what lets `tileiras`
# pick 128-bit accesses. The claim is issued on the parameter itself, never
# after an `offset`.
#
# PR-1 claimed it for an In only (the read path ruling of 10-04), because
# the pre-gate measured the write side of the same claim 45% slower at
# 128x128x32. PR-2 measured what that was (docs/tile-backend-design.md
# 6.25, the write side): not the claim, but `tileiras` budgeting 64 KB of
# shared memory instead of 48 KB for that kernel, one block per SM instead
# of two, which a pointer store with the same claim does as well and which
# the entry hint `occupancy` = 2 undoes. Without the claim an Out's view
# writes 16-bit lanes and is 15% to 27% slower than with it (22% to 27%
# wherever the occupancy is the same); with it, it writes `STG.E.128` and
# matches the pointer store at the same occupancy. So an Out claims it
# too. A Shared has no cells.
const CELL_ALIGN: Int = 16

# The part of the recording the rank-0 rule adds to: the handle counter, the
# region being recorded, the handle table and the spreads and constants made
# so far, by (operand, shape); and, read only, the constants waiting to be
# materialised.
type Grown = {
  next: Int,
  ops: List[TileOp],
  held: Map[Int, Held],
  spreads: Map[String, Int],
  pending: Map[Int, Pend],
}

# A rank-0 constant the body asked for and nothing has used yet (the batch's
# C3': a constant takes no shape). `t_constf` / `t_consti` of shape `[]`
# answer a handle for one of these and record nothing; the first use
# records it, at the shape the use wants, where the use is. So a constant
# multiplied into a `[1024]` tile is one `constant ... : tile<1024xf64>`
# just before the `mulf`, a constant a region's body compares against is a
# rank-0 one inside that region, and a constant nothing reads is not in the
# program at all. The handle the body holds is never defined by an
# operation; every use is renamed to the one materialised for it.
type Pend = {
  dtype: String,
  f: Float,
  i: Int,
  int: Bool,
}

fn grown(next: Int, ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held], spreads: Map[String, Int],
  pending: Map[Int, Pend]) -> Grown =
  Grown { next: next, ops: ops, held: held, spreads: spreads, pending: pending }

# The constant `c` (pending as handle `h`) materialised at `shape`: one
# `ConstF` or `ConstI` of that shape, recorded where the recording stands,
# or the one already made for (`h`, `shape`) while it is visible.
fn materialize(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Int, c: Pend, shape: List[Int]) -> (Grown, Int) = {
  let key = "c${h}:${show(shape)}"
  let known = match map.get(g.spreads, key) {
    Some(k) -> if visible(frames, g.ops, k) { k } else { 0 - 1 }
    None -> 0 - 1
  }
  if known >= 0 {
    (g, known)
  } else {
    let d = g.next
    let made = if c.int { ConstI(d, c.dtype, shape, c.i) } else { ConstF(d, c.dtype, shape, c.f) }
    (Grown { ..g, next: mint(name, g.next, 1), ops: g.ops ++ [made], held: hold(g.held, d, c.dtype, shape),
      spreads: map.insert(g.spreads, key, d) }, d)
  }
}

# `h` where it is used at its own shape: a pending constant materialised at
# rank 0, anything else as it is. Every handle operand that is not an
# element-wise operand (which `widen` takes) goes through here, so that a
# constant's handle never reaches the program undefined.
fn settle(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Int) -> (Grown, Int) =
  match map.get(g.pending, h) {
    Some(c) -> materialize(name, frames, g, h, c, [])
    None -> (g, h)
  }

fn settle_all(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, hs: List[Int]) -> (Grown, List[Int]) = {
  let none: List[Int] = []
  list.fold(hs, (g, none), (acc, h) => {
    let (g1, out) = acc
    let (g2, x) = settle(name, frames, g1, h)
    (g2, out ++ [x])
  })
}

fn settle_opt(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Option[Int]) -> (Grown, Option[Int]) = match h {
  Some(x) -> {
    let (g1, y) = settle(name, frames, g, x)
    (g1, Some(y))
  }
  None -> (g, None)
}

# `widen` of an optional operand (a mask, a pad).
fn widen_opt(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Option[Int], dtype: String,
  shape: List[Int]) -> (Grown, Option[Int]) = match h {
    Some(x) -> {
      let (g1, y) = widen(name, frames, g, x, dtype, shape)
      (g1, Some(y))
    }
    None -> (g, None)
  }

# The operations a region or the entry has at its top level: what an
# operation recorded there now may name.
fn top_of(f: Frame) -> List[TileOp] = match f {
  LoopFrame(outer, _iv, _lower, _upper, _step, _unsigned, _inits, _carried) -> outer
  WhileFrame(outer, _inits, _carried) -> outer
  ReduceFrame(outer, _dim, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> outer
  ScanFrame(outer, _dim, _reverse, _shape, _identities, _operands, _args) -> outer
  ThenFrame(outer, _cond) -> outer
  ElseFrame(outer, _cond, _on_true) -> outer
}

# Whether the handle `h`, which an operation the recording made itself
# defined, may be named where the recording stands: it was defined at the
# top of the region being recorded or of one that encloses it. A spread or a
# cell view made inside a loop body is gone after the loop (the lowering
# refuses a name from a closed region), so the memo below asks this first.
fn visible(frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], h: Int) -> Bool =
  list.any(ops, o => list.contains(defined(o), h))
  || list.any(frames, f => list.any(top_of(f), o => list.contains(defined(o), h)))

# THE RANK-0 RULE. An operand of an element-wise operation that is a rank-0
# tile of the operation's own format, where the operation declares a wider
# shape, is spread to that shape before the operation: the recording
# issues the `Spread` a body would have written with `spread`. Nothing else
# widens on its own: a `[BQ, 1]` against a `[BQ, BK]` is still refused, and
# `broadcast` is how a body says it means it (full numpy broadcasting is
# what the ruling declined: after a reduction that drops a dimension, a
# silent broadcast along the wrong axis is a right-looking wrong answer).
#
# One spread per (operand, shape) while it is visible, so `x - m` and
# `exp(x - m) / s` over one row statistic spread it once. Every program the
# rule changes was refused before it (knife C1's check), so a kernel that
# records today records the same program.
fn widen(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> (Grown, Int) =
  match map.get(g.pending, h) {
    # a constant of the operation's format is made at the operation's
    # shape; one of another format is made at rank 0 and refused as such
    Some(c) -> materialize(name, frames, g, h, c, if c.dtype == dtype { shape } else { [] })
    None -> widen_held(name, frames, g, h, dtype, shape)
  }

fn widen_held(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, h: Int, dtype: String, shape: List[Int]) -> (Grown, Int) = {
  let rank0 = match map.get(g.held, h) {
    Some(x) -> match x.what {
      TileRow -> len(x.shape) == 0 && x.dtype == dtype
      _ -> false
    }
    None -> false
  }
  if not rank0 || len(shape) == 0 {
    (g, h)
  } else {
    let key = "${h}:${show(shape)}"
    let known = match map.get(g.spreads, key) {
      Some(sp) -> if visible(frames, g.ops, sp) { sp } else { 0 - 1 }
      None -> 0 - 1
    }
    if known >= 0 {
      (g, known)
    } else {
      let d = g.next
      (Grown { ..g, next: mint(name, g.next, 1), ops: g.ops ++ [Spread(d, dtype, shape, h)],
        held: hold(g.held, d, dtype, shape), spreads: map.insert(g.spreads, key, d) }, d)
    }
  }
}

# `widen` over every operand of one operation, `dtypes[k]` being what the
# operation declares operand `k` to be.
fn widen_all(name: String, frames: List[Frame], g: Grown, hs: List[Int], dtypes: List[String],
  shape: List[Int]) -> (Grown, List[Int]) = {
  let none: List[Int] = []
  list.fold(range(0, len(hs)), (g, none), (acc, k) => {
    let (g1, out) = acc
    let (g2, h) = widen(name, frames, g1, hs[k], dtypes[k], shape)
    (g2, out ++ [h])
  })
}

# A place that wants a RANK-0 tile of `dtype`: an `if`'s or a loop's
# condition, a loop bound, a reduction's yield, a cell index. Since the
# batch's PR-3 folds `Scalar[D]` into `Tile[D]` the rank is no longer in the
# type, so the recording holds it here (research note `n9`), by number and
# by the call that asked. A handle with no row is not compared, as in
# `check_operand`.
fn check_rank0(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, role: String, h: Int, dtype: String) -> Unit =
  match tile_row(name, frames, ops, held, op, role, h) {
    Some(x) ->
      if x.dtype != dtype || len(x.shape) != 0 {
        refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${role} is ${tile_text(x.dtype, x.shape)}, and it must be the rank-0 ${tile_text(dtype, [])}")
      } else {
        ()
      }
    None -> ()
  }

# Each yield of a reduction or a scan against its operand's format at rank 0.
fn check_yields(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, operands: List[Int], yields: List[Int]) -> Unit = {
  for k in range(0, len(yields)) {
    if k < len(operands) {
      match map.get(held, operands[k]) {
        Some(x) -> check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, op, "yield ${k}", yields[k], x.dtype)
        None -> ()
      }
    } else {
      ()
    }
  }
}

# Each value a region hands back (`outs`) against the one it stands for
# (`ins`): a loop's carried value and what its body continues with, an if's
# two branches. A tile may not change its format or its shape on the way
# round, and since the batch's PR-3 a constant has the shape of its first
# use, so a loop whose starting value is a bare `f_const` and whose body
# answers a [128] tile is the mistake this names (`broadcast` the constant).
# A handle with no row is not compared.
fn check_same_rows(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], held: Map[Int, Held],
  op: String, what: String, ins: List[Int], outs: List[Int]) -> Unit = {
  for k in range(0, len(ins)) {
    if k < len(outs) {
      match (map.get(held, ins[k]), map.get(held, outs[k])) {
        (Some(a), Some(b)) ->
          if a.what == TileRow && b.what == TileRow && (a.dtype != b.dtype || a.shape != b.shape) {
            refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "${what} ${k} is ${tile_text(a.dtype, a.shape)} and comes back ${tile_text(b.dtype, b.shape)}")
          } else {
            ()
          }
        _ -> ()
      }
    } else {
      ()
    }
  }
}

# The cells of parameter `param`, or a refusal naming the call that asked
# for them: a parameter recorded without markers, a `Whole` one and a
# `Shared` one have none.
fn cells_of(name: String, frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], geoms: List[Cells], roles: List[Access],
  param: Int, op: String) -> (List[Int], List[Int], Padding, List[Int]) =
  if len(geoms) == 0 {
    refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "parameter ${param} has no marker; cells are what an In or an Out marker of trace1 to trace5 says, and trace_kernel takes none")
  } else if param < 0 || param >= len(geoms) {
    refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "names parameter ${param}, but ${len(geoms)} parameter(s) are declared")
  } else {
    match geoms[param] {
      Grid(extent, tile, pad, along) -> (extent, tile, pad, along)
      Whole ->
        if roles[param] == Sharing {
          refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "parameter ${param} is a Shared, which has no cells")
        } else {
          refuse_at(name, frames, ops, op, "parameter ${param} is an ${access_word(roles[param])} of the whole tensor, which has no cells")
        }
    }
  }

# What building one cell view adds to the recording.
type Built = {
  next: Int,
  ops: List[TileOp],
  tv: Int,
  view: Int,
  exts: Map[String, Int],
}

# The views parameter `param`'s cells are read and written through: a
# `CellViewOf` over the whole row-major tensor and a partition view of it
# cut into tiles of `cut` (the cells, or a finer cut of them for
# `store_sub`). `tv` is a `CellViewOf` already made and visible for this
# parameter, or -1 to make one. A dynamic extent is the grid's blocks along
# the axis its dimension follows times the tile (6.24), so its operand is
# `num_blocks(k) * tile`, and a stride over a dynamic extent is the product
# that makes it. `exts0` holds the extents of that kind made so far and
# visible here, by "axis:tile", so that two parameters cut the same way
# along one axis share one: vadd's three views are one `num_blocks` and one
# product, not three.
fn cell_view_ops(name: String, next0: Int, param: Int, dtype: String, align: Int, extent: List[Int],
  tile: List[Int], pad: Padding, along: List[Int], cut: List[Int], tv0: Int, exts0: Map[String, Int]) -> Built = {
  var next = next0
  var ops: List[TileOp] = []
  var tv = tv0
  var exts = exts0
  if tv < 0 {
    let r = len(extent)
    var ext_h: List[Int] = []
    var dyn_shape: List[Int] = []
    for j in range(0, r) {
      if extent[j] == DYN_DIM {
        let key = "${along[j]}:${tile[j]}"
        let m = match map.get(exts, key) {
          Some(h) -> h
          None -> {
            let nb = next
            let c = next + 1
            let made = next + 2
            next = mint(name, next, 3)
            ops = ops ++ [NumBlocks(nb, along[j]), IdxConst(c, tile[j]), IdxMul(made, nb, c)]
            exts = map.insert(exts, key, made)
            made
          }
        }
        ext_h = ext_h ++ [m]
        dyn_shape = dyn_shape ++ [m]
      } else {
        ext_h = ext_h ++ [0 - 1]
      }
    }
    # the strides, last dimension first: `stat` times the product of the
    # dynamic extents seen so far (`dyn`, or -1 while there are none)
    var stat = 1
    var dyn = 0 - 1
    var strides: List[Int] = []
    var dyn_strides: List[Int] = []
    for k in range(0, r) {
      let j = r - 1 - k
      if dyn < 0 {
        strides = [stat] ++ strides
      } else if stat == 1 {
        strides = [DYN_DIM] ++ strides
        dyn_strides = [dyn] ++ dyn_strides
      } else {
        let c = next
        let m = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 2)
        ops = ops ++ [IdxConst(c, stat), IdxMul(m, dyn, c)]
        strides = [DYN_DIM] ++ strides
        dyn_strides = [m] ++ dyn_strides
      }
      if extent[j] == DYN_DIM {
        if dyn < 0 {
          dyn = ext_h[j]
        } else {
          let m = next
          next = mint(name, next, 1)
          ops = ops ++ [IdxMul(m, dyn, ext_h[j])]
          dyn = m
        }
      } else {
        stat = stat * extent[j]
      }
    }
    tv = next
    next = mint(name, next, 1)
    ops = ops ++ [CellViewOf(tv, param, dtype, align, extent, strides, dyn_shape, dyn_strides)]
  } else {
    ()
  }
  let pv = next
  next = mint(name, next, 1)
  ops = ops ++ [PartitionViewOf(pv, dtype, tv, cut, range(0, len(cut)), pad)]
  Built { next: next, ops: ops, tv: tv, view: pv, exts: exts }
}

# The entries of `m` whose handle may be named where the recording stands.
fn visible_map(frames: List[Frame], ops: List[TileOp], m: Map[String, Int]) -> Map[String, Int] =
  map.from(list.filter(map.entries(m), e => {
    let (_k, h) = e
    visible(frames, ops, h)
  }))

# This block's cell along each dimension: the block id along the axis a
# dimension follows, or the next of `free` for one that follows none. A
# block id a cell index asked for before is used again while it is visible
# (`blocks`, by axis), so a kernel of three cell reads on one axis issues
# one `get_tile_block_id`; `block_id` itself is not memoised, which keeps
# every program recorded before this the same.
fn cell_index(name: String, frames: List[Frame], here: List[TileOp], next0: Int, blocks0: Map[Int, Int],
  along: List[Int], free: List[Int]) -> (List[Int], Int, List[TileOp], Map[Int, Int]) = {
  var next = next0
  var ops: List[TileOp] = []
  var ix: List[Int] = []
  var blocks = blocks0
  var f = 0
  for j in range(0, len(along)) {
    if along[j] == FREE_AXIS {
      ix = ix ++ [free[f]]
      f = f + 1
    } else {
      let known = match map.get(blocks, along[j]) {
        Some(b) -> if visible(frames, here ++ ops, b) { b } else { 0 - 1 }
        None -> 0 - 1
      }
      if known >= 0 {
        ix = ix ++ [known]
      } else {
        let b = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [BlockId(b, along[j])]
        blocks = map.insert(blocks, along[j], b)
        ix = ix ++ [b]
      }
    }
  }
  (ix, next, ops, blocks)
}

# Every handle `more` defines, held as an index (`i32`, rank 0): the block
# ids and index arithmetic a cell view adds.
fn hold_indices(held: Map[Int, Held], more: List[TileOp]) -> Map[Int, Held] =
  list.fold(more, held, (m, o) => match o {
    BlockId(d, _axis) -> hold(m, d, "i32", [])
    NumBlocks(d, _axis) -> hold(m, d, "i32", [])
    IdxConst(d, _v) -> hold(m, d, "i32", [])
    IdxMul(d, _a, _b) -> hold(m, d, "i32", [])
    _ -> m
  })

## One call the KERNEL made, as the recording saw it, from the side table
## `trace_calls` keeps: the name of the public function (dev.dawn's `called`
## marks), the call it was made inside, and the half-open ranges of
## operations and of `TileProg.globals` it issued, its children's included.
##
## A row is a call the kernel body wrote: one at the top of the body, or one
## inside a closure the kernel handed to a region function (dev.dawn's body
## marks say which those are). What a public function calls on its own
## behalf is not a row: `tile_at`'s `idx_const` and `idx_mul` are
## `tile_at`'s operations. `parent` is the row of the region call whose
## closure this call is in (`d_for`, `d_scan`, ...), or -1 at the top of the
## body, and it is always an EARLIER row: the rows are in the order the
## calls started.
##
## `ops_from` and `ops_to` number operations the way `TileProg.ops` reads
## depth first, a region before its body (the numbering a refusal's `op #k`
## uses), with the entry's `MakeToken(0)` as 0, so the first call of a body
## starts at 1. A call's range holds its children's ranges, and what is left
## of it once they are taken out is what the call issued itself: for a region
## call, the region and its terminator.
pub type Call = {
  name: String,
  parent: Int,
  ops_from: Int,
  ops_to: Int,
  globals_from: Int,
  globals_to: Int,
} derive Show

# What a mark leaves open on the call-mark stack of `trace_calls`: a row of
# the side table (a call the kernel made), a call the library made, a
# closure of the kernel's own, or a closure inside a call the library made.
type Mark =
  | Row(at: Int)
  | Inner
  | KernelBody
  | InnerBody

# The row the innermost open call the kernel made is, or -1.
fn open_row(marks: List[Mark]) -> Int =
  list.fold(marks, 0 - 1, (r, m) => match m {
    Row(at) -> at
    _ -> r
  })

# Whether the innermost open mark is a call the kernel made: a closure run
# there is the kernel's own.
fn in_row(marks: List[Mark]) -> Bool =
  if len(marks) == 0 {
    false
  } else {
    match marks[len(marks) - 1] {
      Row(_at) -> true
      _ -> false
    }
  }

# Whether a call marked now is the kernel's: at the top of the body, or
# directly inside one of its closures.
fn kernel_side(marks: List[Mark]) -> Bool =
  if len(marks) == 0 {
    true
  } else {
    match marks[len(marks) - 1] {
      KernelBody -> true
      _ -> false
    }
  }

## `trace_kernel`, and beside the program the side table of the calls the
## kernel made, in the order they started. The program is the one
## `trace_kernel` answers: it is the same handler, and the marks put nothing
## into `ops`.
##
## With `markers` (one [`erase`]d marker a parameter, and `params` empty) it
## is `trace1` to `trace5`'s recording instead, roles and cells and all, and
## the program is the one they answer: the body then reads its parameters
## with `param(d, k)` in the markers' formats. What the site's call map of a
## kernel recorded with cells runs.
pub fn trace_calls(name: String, params: List[String], body: fn() -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints = [],
  markers: List[Marker] = []) -> (TileProg, List[Call]) =
  if len(markers) == 0 {
    record_calls(name, params, [], [], body, hints)
  } else if len(params) != 0 {
    refuse(name, "`trace_calls` takes formats or markers, not both")
  } else {
    let sigs: List[Sig] = list.map(markers, m => {
      let sg: Sig = m
      sg
    })
    let _args = entry_args(name, sigs)
    record_calls(name, list.map(sigs, s => s.dtype), list.map(sigs, s => s.access), list.map(sigs, s => s.g), body,
      hints)
  }

# `trace_calls` with a role for each parameter (knife C2) and its cells (the
# batch's PR-1): both empty for the two public recorders above, which know
# no roles and refuse nothing on their account and have no cells to read,
# and one per parameter for `trace1` to `trace5`.
fn record_calls(name: String, params: List[String], roles: List[Access], geoms: List[Cells],
  body: fn() -> Unit !Dev, hints: Hints) -> (TileProg, List[Call]) = {
  let (globals, ops, calls) = {
    with handle Dev {
      var next: Int = 1
      var tok: Int = 0
      var ops: List[TileOp] = []
      var globals: List[Global] = []
      var frames: List[Frame] = []
      var marks: List[Mark] = []
      var calls: List[Call] = []
      var held: Map[Int, Held] = map.empty()
      var aims: Map[Int, Int] = map.empty()
      var cell_views: Map[Int, Int] = map.empty()
      var spreads: Map[String, Int] = map.empty()
      var cell_blocks: Map[Int, Int] = map.empty()
      var pending: Map[Int, Pend] = map.empty()
      var cell_tvs: Map[Int, Int] = map.empty()
      var cell_marks: Map[Int, Int] = map.empty()
      var sub_views: Map[String, Int] = map.empty()
      var dyn_exts: Map[String, Int] = map.empty()
      t_call_enter(called_name) => {
        if kernel_side(marks) {
          let at = op_number(frames, ops)
          calls = calls ++ [Call { name: called_name, parent: open_row(marks), ops_from: at, ops_to: at,
            globals_from: len(globals), globals_to: len(globals) }]
          marks = marks ++ [Row(len(calls) - 1)]
        } else {
          marks = marks ++ [Inner]
        }
      }
      t_call_exit() => {
        if len(marks) == 0 { refuse(name, "a call mark closes nothing") } else { () }
        match marks[len(marks) - 1] {
          Row(k) -> {
            let at = op_number(frames, ops)
            let ng = len(globals)
            let rows = calls
            calls = list.map(range(0, len(rows)), j => if j == k { Call { ..rows[j], ops_to: at, globals_to: ng } } else { rows[j] })
          }
          Inner -> ()
          _ -> refuse(name, "a call mark closes a body")
        }
        marks = list.take(marks, len(marks) - 1)
      }
      t_body_enter() => {
        marks = marks ++ [if in_row(marks) { KernelBody } else { InnerBody }]
      }
      t_body_exit() => {
        if len(marks) == 0 { refuse(name, "a body mark closes nothing") } else { () }
        match marks[len(marks) - 1] {
          KernelBody -> ()
          InnerBody -> ()
          _ -> refuse(name, "a body mark closes a call")
        }
        marks = list.take(marks, len(marks) - 1)
      }
      t_block_id(axis) => {
        if axis < 0 || axis > 2 { refuse(name, "block_id axis is 0, 1 or 2, got ${axis}") } else { () }
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [BlockId(d, axis)]
        d
      }
      t_load(param, dtype, base0, shape, strides, mask0, pad0, hints) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a load inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "load")
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_strides(name, shape, strides)
        match (mask0, pad0) {
          (None, Some(_)) -> refuse_at(name, frames, ops, writer(marks, calls, "load"), "a pad with no mask is never read: every lane of the load is")
          _ -> ()
        }
        let (g0, base) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), base0)
        let (g1, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g0, mask0, "i1", shape)
        let (g, pad) = widen_opt(name, frames, g1, pad0, dtype, shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        let t = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 2)
        ops = ops ++ [Load(d, t, param, dtype, base, shape, strides, mask, pad, tok, hints)]
        tok = t
        d
      }
      t_store(param, dtype, base0, shape, strides, v0, mask0, hints) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a store inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "store")
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          refuse_at(name, frames, ops, writer(marks, calls, "store"), "a store writes a tile with a dimension, and this value is rank 0: broadcast it to the shape it is stored as")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_strides(name, shape, strides)
        check_write(name, frames, ops, roles, Some(param), writer(marks, calls, "store"), false)
        let (g0, base) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), base0)
        let (g1, v) = widen(name, frames, g0, v0, dtype, shape)
        let (g, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g1, mask0, "i1", shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads

        let t = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Store(t, param, dtype, base, shape, strides, v, mask, tok, hints)]
        tok = t
        ()
      }
      t_gather(param, dtype, index0, shape, mask0, pad0) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a gather inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "gather")
        check_shape(name, shape)
        let (g0, index) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), index0)
        let (g1, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g0, mask0, "i1", shape)
        let (g, pad) = widen_opt(name, frames, g1, pad0, dtype, shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        let t = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 2)
        ops = ops ++ [Gather(d, t, param, dtype, index, shape, mask, pad, tok)]
        tok = t
        d
      }
      t_scatter(param, dtype, index0, shape, v0, mask0) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a scatter inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "scatter")
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_write(name, frames, ops, roles, Some(param), writer(marks, calls, "scatter"), false)
        let (g0, index) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), index0)
        let (g1, v) = widen(name, frames, g0, v0, dtype, shape)
        let (g, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g1, mask0, "i1", shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let t = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Scatter(t, param, dtype, index, shape, v, mask, tok)]
        tok = t
        ()
      }
      t_atomic_rmw(param, dtype, mode, index0, shape, v0, mask0) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "an atomic inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "atomic")
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_rmw_mode(name, mode)
        check_write(name, frames, ops, roles, Some(param), writer(marks, calls, "atomic_rmw"), false)
        let (g0, index) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), index0)
        let (g1, v) = widen(name, frames, g0, v0, dtype, shape)
        let (g, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g1, mask0, "i1", shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        let t = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 2)
        ops = ops ++ [AtomicRMW(d, t, param, dtype, mode, index, shape, v, mask, tok)]
        tok = t
        d
      }
      t_atomic_cas(param, dtype, index0, shape, cmp0, v0, mask0) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "an atomic inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        check_param(name, params, param, dtype, "atomic")
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_write(name, frames, ops, roles, Some(param), writer(marks, calls, "atomic_cas"), false)
        let (g0, index) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), index0)
        let (g1, cmp) = widen(name, frames, g0, cmp0, dtype, shape)
        let (g2, v) = widen(name, frames, g1, v0, dtype, shape)
        let (g, mask) = widen_opt(name, frames, g2, mask0, "i1", shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        let t = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 2)
        ops = ops ++ [AtomicCAS(d, t, param, dtype, index, shape, cmp, v, mask, tok)]
        tok = t
        d
      }
      t_constf(dtype, shape, value) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          # a constant takes no shape: it is made where it is used (`Pend`)
          pending = map.insert(pending, d, Pend { dtype: dtype, f: value, i: 0, int: false })
        } else {
          ops = ops ++ [ConstF(d, dtype, shape, value)]
        }
        d
      }
      t_consti(dtype, shape, value) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_int_dtype(name, dtype)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          pending = map.insert(pending, d, Pend { dtype: dtype, f: 0.0, i: value, int: true })
        } else {
          ops = ops ++ [ConstI(d, dtype, shape, value)]
        }
        d
      }
      t_iota(n) => {
        check_shape(name, [n])
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [n])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Iota(d, n)]
        d
      }
      t_lanes(base0, shape, strides) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_strides(name, shape, strides)
        let (g, base) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), base0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Lanes(d, base, shape, strides)]
        d
      }
      t_unaryf(op, dtype, shape, a0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_unary(name, op)
        let (g, a) = widen(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), a0, dtype, shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "operand", a, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [UnaryF(d, op, dtype, shape, a)]
        d
      }
      t_binaryf(op, dtype, shape, a0, b0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_binary(name, op)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0], [dtype, dtype], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ws[0]
        let b = ws[1]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "lhs", a, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "rhs", b, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [BinaryF(d, op, dtype, shape, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_fma(dtype, shape, a0, b0, c0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0, c0], [dtype, dtype, dtype], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ws[0]
        let b = ws[1]
        let c = ws[2]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "fma", "lhs", a, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "fma", "rhs", b, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "fma", "accumulator", c, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Fma(d, dtype, shape, a, b, c)]
        d
      }
      t_mmaf(dtype, m, k, n, a0, b0, c0) => {
        check_shape(name, [m, k, n])
        let (g0, ab) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0])
        let (g, c) = widen(name, frames, g0, c0, dtype, [m, n])
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ab[0]
        let b = ab[1]
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmaf", "lhs", a, 1, k)
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmaf", "rhs", b, 0, k)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, [m, n])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [MmaF(d, dtype, m, k, n, a, b, c)]
        d
      }
      t_mmaf_scaled(dtype, scale, dims, a0, b0, c0, sa0, sb0) => {
        # `dims` is [m, k, n, block]; it is one list because an effect
        # operation is a function value and a function value takes at most
        # eight parameters (dev.dawn says so on the declaration).
        if len(dims) != 4 {
          refuse(name, "mmaf_scaled takes [m, k, n, block], got ${show(dims)}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        let m = dims[0]
        let k = dims[1]
        let n = dims[2]
        let block = dims[3]
        # the four shapes the dialect derives from these numbers, checked
        # here for the reason every other shape in this file is: a tile
        # dimension is a power of two, and `k / block` is one of them.
        if block <= 0 || k % block != 0 {
          refuse(name, "mmaf_scaled over K=${k} in blocks of ${block}, which does not divide it")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        check_shape(name, [m, k, n, k / block])
        let (g0, ab) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0, sa0, sb0])
        let (g, c) = widen(name, frames, g0, c0, "f32", [m, n])
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ab[0]
        let b = ab[1]
        let sa = ab[2]
        let sb = ab[3]
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmaf_scaled", "lhs", a, 1, k)
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmaf_scaled", "rhs", b, 0, k)
        held = hold(held, next, "f32", [m, n])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [MmaFScaled(d, dtype, scale, m, k, n, block, a, b, c, sa, sb)]
        d
      }
      t_mmai(m, k, n, a0, b0, c0) => {
        check_shape(name, [m, k, n])
        let (g0, ab) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0])
        let (g, c) = widen(name, frames, g0, c0, "i32", [m, n])
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ab[0]
        let b = ab[1]
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmai", "lhs", a, 1, k)
        check_k(name, frames, ops, held, "mmai", "rhs", b, 0, k)
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [m, n])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [MmaI(d, m, k, n, a, b, c)]
        d
      }
      t_cmpf(pred, dtype, shape, a0, b0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_pred(name, pred)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0], [dtype, dtype], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ws[0]
        let b = ws[1]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "cmpf", "lhs", a, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "cmpf", "rhs", b, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, "i1", shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [CmpF(d, pred, dtype, shape, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_cmpi(pred, shape, a0, b0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_int_pred(name, pred)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0], ["i32", "i32"], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ws[0]
        let b = ws[1]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "cmpi", "lhs", a, "i32", shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "cmpi", "rhs", b, "i32", shape)
        held = hold(held, next, "i1", shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [CmpI(d, pred, shape, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_unaryi(op, dtype, shape, a0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_unary_int(name, op)
        check_int_dtype(name, dtype)
        let (g, a) = widen(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), a0, dtype, shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "operand", a, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [UnaryI(d, op, dtype, shape, a)]
        d
      }
      t_binaryi(op, dtype, shape, a0, b0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_binary_int(name, op)
        check_int_dtype(name, dtype)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0], [dtype, dtype], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ws[0]
        let b = ws[1]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "lhs", a, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "rhs", b, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [BinaryI(d, op, dtype, shape, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_convert(op, from, to, shape, a0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_convert(name, op)
        check_ftof(name, op, from, to)
        let (g, a) = widen(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), a0, from, shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, op, "operand", a, from, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, to, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Convert(d, op, from, to, shape, a)]
        d
      }
      t_repack(op, from, to, shape, a0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_repack(name, op, from, to, shape)
        let (g, a) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), a0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        held = hold(held, next, to, repack_shape(name, from, to, shape))
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Repack(d, op, from, to, shape, repack_shape(name, from, to, shape), a)]
        d
      }
      t_select(dtype, shape, cond0, a0, b0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        let (g, ws) = widen_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [cond0, a0, b0], ["i1", dtype, dtype], shape)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let cond = ws[0]
        let a = ws[1]
        let b = ws[2]
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "select", "condition", cond, "i1", shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "select", "value if true", a, dtype, shape)
        check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "select", "value if false", b, dtype, shape)
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Select(d, dtype, shape, cond, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_idx_const(value) => {
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [IdxConst(d, value)]
        d
      }
      t_idx_add(a0, b0) => {
        let (g, ab) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0])
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ab[0]
        let b = ab[1]
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [IdxAdd(d, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_idx_mul(a0, b0) => {
        let (g, ab) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [a0, b0])
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let a = ab[0]
        let b = ab[1]
        held = hold(held, next, "i32", [])
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [IdxMul(d, a, b)]
        d
      }
      t_loop_begin(lower0, upper0, step0, unsigned, inits0) => {
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a loop inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        let (g, hs) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [lower0, upper0, step0] ++ inits0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let lower = hs[0]
        let upper = hs[1]
        let step = hs[2]
        let inits = list.drop(hs, 3)
        let bounded = writer(marks, calls, "for")
        check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, bounded, "lower bound", lower, "i32")
        check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, bounded, "upper bound", upper, "i32")
        check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, bounded, "step", step, "i32")
        if len(frames) >= MAX_LOOP_DEPTH {
          refuse(name, "loops nest deeper than ${MAX_LOOP_DEPTH}; a body that recurses through d_for unrolls forever")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        # the token rides along as the last carried value
        let all = inits ++ [tok]
        let iv = next
        let base = next + 1
        next = mint(name, next, 1 + len(all))
        let carried = list.map(range(0, len(all)), k => base + k)
        held = copy_held(hold(held, iv, "i32", []), all, carried)
        frames = frames ++ [LoopFrame(ops, iv, lower, upper, step, unsigned, all, carried)]
        ops = []
        tok = last(carried)
        (iv, but_last(carried))
      }
      t_loop_end(outs0) => {
        if len(frames) == 0 { refuse(name, "loop_end with no loop open") } else { () }
        let (g, outs) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), outs0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        match frames[len(frames) - 1] {
          LoopFrame(saved, iv, lower, upper, step, unsigned, inits, carried) -> {
            let want = len(inits) - 1
            if len(outs) != want {
              refuse(name, "a loop body carrying ${want} value(s) answered ${len(outs)}")
            } else {
              ()
            }
            check_same_rows(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "for"), "carried value", carried, outs)
            let (outer, inner) = (saved, ops)
            let base = next
            next = mint(name, next, len(inits))
            let results = list.map(range(0, len(inits)), k => base + k)
            let body = inner ++ [Continue(outs ++ [tok])]
            held = copy_held(held, inits, results)
            frames = list.take(frames, len(frames) - 1)
            ops = outer ++ [For(iv, lower, upper, step, unsigned, inits, carried, results, body)]
            tok = last(results)
            but_last(results)
          }
          _ -> refuse(name, "loop_end closes a region that is not a loop")
        }
      }
      t_while_begin(inits0) => {
        let (g, inits) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), inits0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, is_pure_region) { refuse(name, "a loop inside a reduce, scan or if region has no memory order") } else { () }
        if len(frames) >= MAX_LOOP_DEPTH {
          refuse(name, "loops nest deeper than ${MAX_LOOP_DEPTH}; a body that recurses through d_loop unrolls forever")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        # the token rides along as the last carried value, as `for`'s does
        let all = inits ++ [tok]
        let base = next
        next = mint(name, next, len(all))
        let carried = list.map(range(0, len(all)), k => base + k)
        held = copy_held(held, all, carried)
        frames = frames ++ [WhileFrame(ops, all, carried)]
        ops = []
        tok = last(carried)
        but_last(carried)
      }
      t_while_end(cond0, outs0) => {
        if len(frames) == 0 { refuse(name, "while_end with no loop open") } else { () }
        let (g, hs) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [cond0] ++ outs0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let cond = hs[0]
        let outs = list.drop(hs, 1)
        check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "loop"), "exit condition", cond, "i1")
        match frames[len(frames) - 1] {
          WhileFrame(saved, inits, carried) -> {
            let want = len(inits) - 1
            if len(outs) != want {
              refuse(name, "a loop body carrying ${want} value(s) answered ${len(outs)}")
            } else {
              ()
            }
            check_same_rows(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "loop"), "carried value", carried, outs)
            # spelled as two bindings rather than one tuple, and the token
            # through a name: `for`'s arm above is what two of run.sh's
            # mutants anchor on, and an anchor that matched here as well
            # would stop being unique
            let inner = ops
            let outer = saved
            let base = next
            next = mint(name, next, len(inits))
            let results = list.map(range(0, len(inits)), k => base + k)
            # the exit: break with what entered this iteration (the token as
            # it stands now), or fall through to the continue below
            let exit = If([], cond, [Break(but_last(carried) ++ [tok])], [Yield([])])
            let body = inner ++ [exit, Continue(outs ++ [tok])]
            held = copy_held(held, inits, results)
            frames = list.take(frames, len(frames) - 1)
            ops = outer ++ [Loop(inits, carried, results, body)]
            let after_loop = last(results)
            tok = after_loop
            but_last(results)
          }
          _ -> refuse(name, "while_end closes a region that is not a loop")
        }
      }
      t_return_if(cond0) => {
        let (g, cond) = settle(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), cond0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        check_rank0(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "return"), "condition", cond, "i1")
        # ReturnOp::verify walks up through `if` and `loop` (13.4 added the
        # second) and must reach the entry; a `for`, a reduction or a scan on
        # the way is refused by the dialect, so it is refused here
        let open = frames
        if list.any(open, f => not return_passes(f)) {
          refuse(name, "a return inside a for, reduce or scan region; only an if or a loop may enclose one")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        ops = ops ++ [If([], cond, [Return], [Yield([])])]
      }
      t_spread(dtype, shape, src) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          refuse(name, "spread widens a rank-0 tile to a shape, and the rank-0 shape is not one")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        match map.get(pending, src) {
          # a constant spread is the constant made at that shape
          Some(c) -> {
            let (g, made) = materialize(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), src, c, if c.dtype == dtype { shape } else { [] })
            next = g.next
            ops = g.ops
            held = g.held
            spreads = g.spreads
            check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "spread", "operand", made, dtype, shape)
            made
          }
          None -> {
            check_operand(name, frames, ops, held, "spread", "operand", src, dtype, [])
            held = hold(held, next, dtype, shape)
            let d = next
            next = mint(name, next, 1)
            ops = ops ++ [Spread(d, dtype, shape, src)]
            d
          }
        }
      }
      t_reduce_begin(dim, shape, identities, operands0) => {
        let (g, operands) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), operands0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        if len(frames) >= MAX_LOOP_DEPTH {
          refuse(name, "regions nest deeper than ${MAX_LOOP_DEPTH}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        check_shape(name, shape)
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          refuse(name, "a reduction reduces a tile with a dimension, not a rank-0 one")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if dim < 0 || dim >= len(shape) {
          refuse(name, "a rank-${len(shape)} tile has dimensions 0 to ${len(shape) - 1} to reduce along, got dim=${dim}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if len(identities) != len(operands) {
          refuse(name, "a reduction over ${len(operands)} operand(s) carries ${len(identities)} identity/identities")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if len(operands) == 0 { refuse(name, "a reduction with no operands") } else { () }
        let base = next
        next = mint(name, next, 2 * len(operands))
        let args = list.map(range(0, 2 * len(operands)), k => base + k)
        held = args_held(held, operands, args)
        frames = frames ++ [ReduceFrame(ops, dim, shape, identities, operands, args)]
        ops = []
        args
      }
      t_reduce_end(yields0) => {
        if len(frames) == 0 { refuse(name, "reduce_end with no reduction open") } else { () }
        let (g, yields) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), yields0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        match frames[len(frames) - 1] {
          ReduceFrame(outer, dim, shape, identities, operands, args) -> {
            if len(yields) != len(operands) {
              refuse(name, "a reduction over ${len(operands)} operand(s) yielded ${len(yields)}")
            } else {
              ()
            }
            check_yields(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "reduce"), operands, yields)
            let inner = ops
            let base = next
            next = mint(name, next, len(operands))
            let results = list.map(range(0, len(operands)), k => base + k)
            held = reduce_held(held, operands, dim, results)
            frames = list.take(frames, len(frames) - 1)
            ops = outer ++ [Reduce(results, dim, shape, identities, operands, args, inner ++ [Yield(yields)])]
            results
          }
          _ -> refuse(name, "reduce_end closes a region that is not a reduction")
        }
      }
      t_scan_begin(dim, reverse, shape, identities, operands0) => {
        let (g, operands) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), operands0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        if len(frames) >= MAX_LOOP_DEPTH {
          refuse(name, "regions nest deeper than ${MAX_LOOP_DEPTH}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        check_shape(name, shape)
        if len(shape) == 0 {
          refuse(name, "a scan scans a tile with a dimension, not a rank-0 one")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if dim < 0 || dim >= len(shape) {
          refuse(name, "a rank-${len(shape)} tile has dimensions 0 to ${len(shape) - 1} to scan along, got dim=${dim}")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if len(identities) != len(operands) {
          refuse(name, "a scan over ${len(operands)} operand(s) carries ${len(identities)} identity/identities")
        } else {
          ()
        }
        if len(operands) == 0 { refuse(name, "a scan with no operands") } else { () }
        let base = next
        next = mint(name, next, 2 * len(operands))
        let args = list.map(range(0, 2 * len(operands)), k => base + k)
        held = args_held(held, operands, args)
        frames = frames ++ [ScanFrame(ops, dim, reverse, shape, identities, operands, args)]
        ops = []
        args
      }
      t_scan_end(yields0) => {
        if len(frames) == 0 { refuse(name, "scan_end with no scan open") } else { () }
        let (g, yields) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), yields0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        match frames[len(frames) - 1] {
          ScanFrame(outer, dim, reverse, shape, identities, operands, args) -> {
            if len(yields) != len(operands) {
              refuse(name, "a scan over ${len(operands)} operand(s) yielded ${len(yields)}")
            } else {
              ()
            }
            check_yields(name, frames, ops, held, writer(marks, calls, "scan"), operands, yields)
            let inner = ops
            let base = next
            next = mint(name, next, len(operands))
            let results = list.map(range(0, len(operands)), k => base + k)
            held = copy_held(held, operands, results)
            frames = list.take(frames, len(frames) - 1)
            ops = outer ++ [Scan(results, dim, reverse, shape, identities, operands, args, inner ++ [Yield(yields)])]
            results
          }
          _ -> refuse(name, "scan_end closes a region that is not a scan")
        }
      }
      t_extract(dtype, from, to, src0, indices0) => {
        let (g, hs) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [src0] ++ indices0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let src = hs[0]
        let indices = list.drop(hs, 1)
        check_shape(name, from)
        check_shape(name, to)
        check_extract(name, from, to, len(indices))
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, to)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Extract(d, dtype, from, to, src, indices)]
        d
      }
      t_insert(dtype, sub, whole, src0, dst0, indices0) => {
        let (g, hs) = settle_all(name, frames, grown(next, ops, held, spreads, pending), [src0, dst0] ++ indices0)
        next = g.next
        ops = g.ops
        held = g.held
        spreads = g.spreads
        let src = hs[0]
        let dst = hs[1]
        let indices = list.drop(hs, 2)
        check_shape(name, sub)
        check_shape(name, whole)
        check_insert(name, sub, whole, len(indices))
        held = hold(held, next, dtype, whole)
        let d = next
        next = mint(name, next, 1)
        ops = ops ++ [Insert(d, dtype, sub, whole, src, dst, indices)]
        d
      }
      t_powi(dtype, exp_dtype, shape, a0, n0) => {
        check_shape(name, shape)
        check_powi(name, dtype, exp_dtype)
